OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026", "title": "Constant-factor hardness of Min-UnCut", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "102", "family_title": "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds", "abstract_html": "For every fixed <i>C</i> &amp;gt; 1, approximating Min-UnCut within factor <i>C</i> is NP-hard, even on simple undirected unweighted graphs.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a finite simple undirected graph \\(G=(V,E)\\), define …", "statement": "<p>For a finite simple undirected graph \\(G=(V,E)\\), define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{uncut}_G(S)\n=\n|\\{uv\\in E:\\ u,v\\in S\\text{ or }u,v\\notin S\\}|,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\n=\n\\min_{S\\subseteq V}\\operatorname{uncut}_G(S).\n\\]\nThe parts of the bipartition may be empty and need not be balanced. Equivalently, \\(\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\\) is the minimum number of edges whose deletion makes \\(G\\) bipartite.</p><p>For every fixed integer \\(K\\ge2\\), there is a deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction mapping a \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) formula \\(\\varphi\\) to a pair \\((G,k)\\), where \\(G\\) is an explicit finite simple undirected unweighted graph and \\(k\\ge1\\) is a binary-encoded integer, such that\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ satisfiable}\n\\implies\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\\le k,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ unsatisfiable}\n\\implies\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)&gt;Kk.\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(K\\), the graph size, full construction work, and bit complexity are polynomial in the bit length of \\(\\varphi\\). All constants are effective; no advice or oracle is supplied to the reduction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(K\\ge2\\) and set \\(J=10K\\). Given \\(\\varphi\\), use Proposition \\(2\\) with the rational completeness error selected in Proposition \\(6\\), obtaining a nonempty uniform equation list of length \\(M_0\\).</p><p>Perform the deterministic finite replacement of Proposition \\(7\\), then the graph realization of Proposition \\(8\\). Compute\n\\[\nD_0=C_KM_0^t\n\\]\nand output\n\\[\n(G,k),\\qquad k=5D_0.\n\\]\nThis is a positive integer computed without knowing the optimum.</p><p>In the YES case,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\n&lt;\n\\frac92D_0\n&lt;\n5D_0=k.\n\\]\nIn the NO case,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\n&gt;\n\\left(10K-\\frac12\\right)D_0\n&gt;\n5KD_0\n=\nKk.\n\\]</p><p>All parameter searches use the displayed effective inequalities. Rational integrations, cell enumeration, and sign-table construction are algorithms performed by the reduction itself; their work is a finite constant for fixed \\(K\\), not numerical advice. Proposition \\(2\\) has polynomial work and output length, and Propositions \\(7\\)–\\(8\\) give polynomial subsequent work and output length. The integer \\(k\\) has \\(O_K(1+\\log M_0)\\) bits. This proves the deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction and all graph and threshold assertions.</p><p>For any fixed real \\(C&gt;1\\), choose a fixed integer \\(K\\ge\\max\\{2,C\\}\\). A deterministic polynomial-time algorithm returning a bipartition of cost at most \\(C\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\\) would decide \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\): accept exactly when its returned cost is at most \\(Kk\\). In the YES case the cost is at most \\(Ck\\le Kk\\); in the NO case every bipartition costs more than \\(Kk\\). Thus such an algorithm implies \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).</p><p>For a randomized approximation with fixed positive success probability, validate the returned bipartition and apply the same test. A NO instance is never accepted. A YES instance is accepted whenever approximation succeeds; a fixed number of independent repetitions raises acceptance probability to at least \\(1/2\\), if necessary. Thus the corresponding randomized consequence is \\(\\mathrm{NP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{RP}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In minimum \\(2\\mathrm{CNF}\\) clause deletion, the input is an explicit list of two-literal disjunctions, each of unit cost.", "statement": "<p>In minimum \\(2\\mathrm{CNF}\\) clause deletion, the input is an explicit list of two-literal disjunctions, each of unit cost. Clause occurrences, rather than distinct clauses, are counted. Let \\(\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{del}}(F)\\) be the minimum number of clause occurrences whose deletion makes \\(F\\) satisfiable.</p><p>For every fixed real \\(C&gt;1\\), minimum \\(2\\mathrm{CNF}\\) clause deletion is NP-hard to approximate within factor \\(C\\). In particular, a deterministic polynomial-time \\(C\\)-approximation would imply \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Given \\(G=(V,E)\\), introduce a Boolean variable \\(x_v\\) for each vertex and, for every edge \\(uv\\), the two clauses\n\\[\n(x_u\\lor x_v),\n\\qquad\n(\\neg x_u\\lor\\neg x_v).\n\\]\nCall the resulting formula \\(F_G\\).</p><p>Equal endpoint values falsify exactly one clause, whereas unequal endpoint values satisfy both. Therefore, for every assignment \\(a\\), with\n\\[\nS_a=\\{v:a(x_v)=1\\},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{unsat}(F_G,a)=\\operatorname{uncut}_G(S_a).\n\\]</p><p>Deleting an assignment’s false clauses gives a feasible deletion set of the same size. Conversely, from any feasible deletion set \\(D\\), a polynomial-time \\(2\\mathrm{SAT}\\) algorithm finds an assignment \\(a\\) satisfying \\(F_G\\setminus D\\). Every original clause falsified by \\(a\\) belongs to \\(D\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{uncut}_G(S_a)\n=\n\\operatorname{unsat}(F_G,a)\n\\le |D|.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{del}}(F_G)\n=\n\\min_a\\operatorname{unsat}(F_G,a)\n=\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G).\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(C&gt;1\\), choose a fixed integer \\(K\\ge\\max\\{2,C\\}\\), and apply Theorem \\(1\\). The formula \\(F_G\\) has \\(|V|\\) variables and \\(2|E|\\) clauses and preserves the same threshold gap:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{del}}(F_G)\\le k\n\\quad\\text{in the YES case},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{del}}(F_G)&gt;Kk\n\\quad\\text{in the NO case}.\n\\]\nA \\(C\\)-approximation returns a feasible deletion set of size at most \\(Ck\\le Kk\\) in the former case, whereas every feasible deletion set has size greater than \\(Kk\\) in the latter. Comparing the returned size with \\(Kk\\) decides \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) in polynomial time.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Affine Fourier and Gaussian preliminaries.", "statement": "<p>Affine Fourier and Gaussian preliminaries.</p><p>All finite-set averages and Fourier transforms use probability normalization. Every affine space below is finite, nonempty, and over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\).</p><p>For an affine space \\(A\\), let \\(D(A)\\) be the vector space of affine functions \\(A\\to\\mathbb F_2\\), including constants. Write \\(1\\in D(A)\\) for the constant-one function. For \\(\\alpha\\in D(A)^*\\), set\n\\[\n\\chi_\\alpha(b)=(-1)^{\\alpha(b)}.\n\\]\nCall \\(\\alpha\\) odd if \\(\\alpha(1)=1\\).</p><p>For a real function \\(v\\) on a finite binary vector space \\(W\\), write\n\\[\n\\widehat v(\\alpha)=\\mathbb E_{w\\in W}v(w)(-1)^{\\alpha(w)}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\gamma_d\\) be standard Gaussian probability measure on \\(\\mathbb R^d\\). Define the normalized Hermite polynomials by\n\\[\ne^{tz-t^2/2}\n=\n\\sum_{j\\ge0}H_j(z)\\frac{t^j}{\\sqrt{j!}},\n\\]\nand the product Hermite functions by\n\\[\n\\psi_I(c)=\\prod_x H_{I_x}(c_x),\n\\qquad |I|=\\sum_x I_x.\n\\]</p><p>The following facts hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Labels of \\(A\\) are in bijection with odd frequencies of \\(D(A)\\), and this bijection respects affine maps.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Fourier inversion, Parseval’s identity, the fourth-moment identity, and the stated rule for pullback of Fourier coefficients hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The product Hermite functions form an orthonormal basis; the Gaussian energy, noise, differentiation, and sign identities in Lemma \\(1.3\\) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(1.1\\) supplies the label-frequency correspondence and its compatibility with affine maps. Lemma \\(1.2\\) supplies the finite Fourier identities, including pullback without an injectivity or surjectivity assumption. Lemma \\(1.3\\) supplies the Gaussian identities used in the smoothing and honest-proof calculations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Odd frequencies are labels.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the affine setting of Proposition \\(1\\), define \\( \\operatorname{ev}_a(b)=b(a) \\qquad(a\\in A,\\ b\\in D(A)). \\) The map \\(a\\mapsto\\operatorname{ev}_a\\) is a bijection from \\(A\\) onto the odd frequencies of \\(D(A)\\).", "statement": "<p>In the affine setting of Proposition \\(1\\), define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ev}_a(b)=b(a)\n\\qquad(a\\in A,\\ b\\in D(A)).\n\\]\nThe map \\(a\\mapsto\\operatorname{ev}_a\\) is a bijection from \\(A\\) onto the odd frequencies of \\(D(A)\\).</p><p>If \\(\\pi:A\\to A'\\) is affine and\n\\[\n\\pi^*:D(A')\\to D(A),\\qquad b\\mapsto b\\circ\\pi,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n(\\pi^*)^*(\\operatorname{ev}_a)=\\operatorname{ev}_{\\pi(a)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose affine coordinates \\(A=\\mathbb F_2^r\\). Every affine function is\n\\[\nb(x)=b_0+\\sum_{j=1}^r b_jx_j.\n\\]\nA functional taking the constant function to one is uniquely\n\\[\nb\\longmapsto b_0+\\sum_j b_ja_j\n\\]\nfor some \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_2^r\\). Finally,\n\\[\n(\\pi^*)^*(\\operatorname{ev}_a)(b)=b(\\pi(a)).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the Fourier setting of Proposition \\(1\\), \\( v(w)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in W^*}\\widehat v(\\alpha)(-1)^{\\alpha(w)}, \\qquad \\sum_\\alpha|\\widehat v(\\alpha)|^2=\\mathbb E v^2. \\) If \\(T:W'\\to W\\) is linear, each character of \\(v\\) …", "statement": "<p>In the Fourier setting of Proposition \\(1\\),\n\\[\nv(w)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in W^*}\\widehat v(\\alpha)(-1)^{\\alpha(w)},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_\\alpha|\\widehat v(\\alpha)|^2=\\mathbb E v^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(T:W'\\to W\\) is linear, each character of \\(v\\) pulls back to its image under \\(T^*\\); coefficients with the same image add. Injectivity and surjectivity of \\(T\\) are unnecessary.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\n\\sum_\\alpha|\\widehat v(\\alpha)|^4\n=\n\\mathbb E_{a,b,c\\in W}v(a)v(b)v(c)v(a+b+c).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Character orthogonality gives inversion and Parseval’s identity. For a character indexed by \\(\\alpha\\),\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\alpha(Tw')}=(-1)^{(T^*\\alpha)(w')},\n\\]\nso frequencies with the same image contribute to the same coefficient.</p><p>For (2.1), expand the four factors and average over the three independent points. Orthogonality forces all four frequencies to agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the Gaussian setting of Proposition \\(1\\), the following identities and inequalities hold.", "statement": "<p>In the Gaussian setting of Proposition \\(1\\), the following identities and inequalities hold.</p><p>For a function \\(F\\) with square-integrable weak gradient,\n\\[\n\\int\\|\\nabla F\\|^2\\,d\\gamma_d\n=\n\\sum_I |I|\\,|\\widehat F(I)|^2.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nIn particular, a mean-zero \\(F\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\int F^2\\,d\\gamma_d\n\\le\n\\int\\|\\nabla F\\|^2\\,d\\gamma_d.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\rho&lt;1\\), the Gaussian noise operator\n\\[\nT_\\rho Q(c)=\\mathbb E_g Q\\!\\left(\\rho c+\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}\\,g\\right)\n\\]\nmultiplies \\(\\psi_I\\) by \\(\\rho^{|I|}\\).</p><p>If \\(Q\\) is bounded and \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), Gaussian convolution is smooth and\n\\[\n\\partial_{c_x}\\mathbb E_g Q(c+\\sigma g)\n=\n\\frac1\\sigma\\mathbb E_g g_xQ(c+\\sigma g).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nAn additional independent average may be included in \\(Q\\).</p><p>If \\(u,v\\) are unit vectors and \\(g\\) is standard isotropic Gaussian, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P[\\operatorname{sgn}\\langle g,u\\rangle\n\\ne\\operatorname{sgn}\\langle g,v\\rangle]\n=\n\\frac{\\angle(u,v)}{\\pi}\n\\le \\|u-v\\|.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nIn particular, adding an independent centered Gaussian of standard deviation \\(a\\) to a standard Gaussian changes its sign with probability\n\\[\n\\frac{\\arctan(a)}{\\pi}\\le a.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Gaussian integration of the product of two generating functions proves orthonormality of the \\(H_j\\). Their products form an orthonormal basis of \\(L^2(\\gamma_d)\\). For completeness, if a function is orthogonal to all these polynomials, its Gaussian-weighted exponential transform has every Taylor coefficient zero. Cauchy–Schwarz makes that transform entire in each variable; its restriction to imaginary arguments is the Fourier transform of an integrable function, which is therefore zero. Thus the original function is zero almost everywhere.</p><p>Differentiating the generating function gives\n\\[\nH'_j=\\sqrt j\\,H_{j-1}.\n\\]\nThis proves (2.2) first for polynomials and then by passage to Hermite partial sums and weak derivatives. For the smoothed functions used below, the weighted series converges directly. The mean-zero inequality follows because every nonconstant Hermite term has degree at least one.</p><p>Averaging the generating function proves the multiplier rule for \\(T_\\rho\\). Differentiating the Gaussian density gives (2.3); its integrable derivatives justify differentiation.</p><p>For (2.4), use rotational symmetry in the two-dimensional plane spanned by \\(u,v\\). The collinear cases follow by continuity. The final assertion follows by applying this calculation to the original Gaussian and its independent Gaussian perturbation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Unconditional starting gap and uniform repetition.", "statement": "<p>Unconditional starting gap and uniform repetition.</p><p>For every fixed rational \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), there is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to a nonempty explicit uniform list of equations\n\\[\nx_i+x_j+x_k=b\n\\qquad\\text{over }\\mathbb F_2\n\\]\nsuch that satisfiable inputs admit an assignment satisfying at least \\(1-\\zeta\\) of the list, whereas unsatisfiable inputs admit none satisfying more than \\(3/4\\). The list length and full bit complexity are polynomial for fixed \\(\\zeta\\).</p><p>There is also an effectively specified universal constant \\(c&lt;1\\) such that every finite two-player game with at most eight first-player answers, at most two second-player answers, and value at most \\(11/12\\), has ordinary \\(r\\)-fold repeated value at most \\(c^r\\), for every integer \\(r\\ge0\\). The same \\(c\\) works for all question distributions and question-set sizes.</p><p>A finite game’s value is the maximum acceptance probability over separate deterministic response functions. Independent shared or private randomness does not increase this maximum. Ordinary repetition samples independent question pairs and accepts only when every coordinate accepts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(2.1\\) gives the uniform near-satisfiable equation gap. Lemma \\(2.2\\) gives the uniform repeated-game bound.</p><p>For the assertion about randomness, fixing the independent shared and private random seeds realizes at least the average success probability of a randomized strategy. Thus the deterministic value remains an upper bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Theorem 2.2, “An unconditional equation gap.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed rational \\(\\zeta>0\\), the equation-list reduction asserted in Proposition \\(2\\) exists, is deterministic, and has polynomial output length and bit complexity.", "statement": "<p>For every fixed rational \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), the equation-list reduction asserted in Proposition \\(2\\) exists, is deterministic, and has polynomial output length and bit complexity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Håstad’s Theorem 5.4, including the verifier-to-equations construction in its proof, gives a deterministic polynomial construction of rationally weighted three-variable equations with YES value at least \\(1-\\delta\\) and NO value at most \\((1+\\delta)/2\\), for every fixed sufficiently small dyadic \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). The weights and their polynomial-size support are explicit.</p><p>For rational weights \\((w_1,\\ldots,w_r)\\) and an integer \\(Q\\), take \\(\\lfloor Qw_j\\rfloor\\) copies of equation \\(j\\), and distribute the remaining copies among the largest fractional remainders. The resulting probabilities differ from the original ones in total variation by at most \\(r/(2Q)\\). Floors, comparisons, and sorting require polynomial bit complexity.</p><p>Choose fixed \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\delta+\\varepsilon&lt;\\zeta,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{1+\\delta}{2}+\\varepsilon&lt;\\frac34,\n\\]\nand choose \\(Q\\ge r/(2\\varepsilon)\\) with \\(Q=O_\\varepsilon(r)\\). Satisfaction probabilities change by at most \\(\\varepsilon\\) for every assignment simultaneously. This gives the required uniform list. The underlying construction can be taken nonempty; exceptional bounded input cases are decidable directly and replaced by fixed YES or NO lists.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Theorem 2.3, “Uniform parallel repetition.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every finite game satisfying the answer-size and value assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\) has \\(r\\)-fold repeated value at most \\(c^r\\), where one may take \\( c= \\left(1-\\frac{1}{6000\\cdot12^3}\\right)^{1/4}<1. \\)", "statement": "<p>Every finite game satisfying the answer-size and value assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\) has \\(r\\)-fold repeated value at most \\(c^r\\), where one may take\n\\[\nc=\n\\left(1-\\frac{1}{6000\\cdot12^3}\\right)^{1/4}&lt;1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is an instance of ordinary parallel repetition. More explicitly, after padding the answer sets to sizes eight and two, Holenstein’s Theorem 2.5 permits the displayed constant. That theorem imposes no regularity condition on the joint question distribution.</p><p>A rational upper bound strictly between \\(c\\) and one may be used in every effective parameter choice below.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.4, “Low-degree correlation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A folded bit proof on a nonempty affine alphabet \\(A\\) is a function \\( f:\\{P:A\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\}\\to\\{1,-1\\}, \\qquad f(-P)=-f(P). \\) Queries are Boolean functions themselves:", "statement": "<p>A folded bit proof on a nonempty affine alphabet \\(A\\) is a function\n\\[\nf:\\{P:A\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\}\\to\\{1,-1\\},\n\\qquad f(-P)=-f(P).\n\\]\nQueries are Boolean functions themselves: equal functions on \\(A\\) receive equal answers. Evaluation at a label \\(a\\in A\\) is the proof \\(f(P)=P(a)\\).</p><p>Allow a finite random index \\(\\xi\\), with arbitrary probability weights, to select \\(A_\\xi,f_\\xi\\). Conditional on \\(\\xi\\), all sampling below is on \\(A_\\xi\\). All unspecified expectations include \\(\\xi\\).</p><p>For integers \\(m,n\\ge2\\), put\n\\[\nX=[n]^m,\\qquad N=n^m,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal R\n=\n\\{(i,y):i\\in[m],\\ y\\in[n]^{[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}}\\},\n\\qquad M=mn^{m-1}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\in X\\), write \\(x_{-i}\\) for the tuple with coordinate \\(i\\) deleted. Sample the rows\n\\[\nB_y^i\\in D(A)\n\\qquad((i,y)\\in\\mathcal R)\n\\]\nindependently and uniformly, and define\n\\[\nB(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^m B_{x_{-i}}^i,\n\\qquad\nB(a)=(B(x)(a))_{x\\in X}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nDefine the face code\n\\[\n\\mathcal C\n=\n\\left\\{\nz\\in\\mathbb F_2^X:\nz_x=\\sum_{i=1}^m z^i_{x_{-i}}\n\\text{ for some }\nz^i\\in\\mathbb F_2^{[n]^{[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}}}\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThen \\(B(a)\\in\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>For \\(u\\in\\mathbb R^X,d\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal C}\\), define\n\\[\nP_{u,d}(z)\n=\n\\operatorname{sgn}\n\\left(\n\\frac1{\\sqrt N}\\sum_{x\\in X}u_x(-1)^{z_x}+d_z\n\\right).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nUse a fixed convention at zero. Its pullback is \\(a\\mapsto P_{u,d}(B(a))\\). For a table \\(P\\) on \\(\\mathcal C\\), let \\(P^{z,+}\\) and \\(P^{z,-}\\) be obtained by forcing its entry at \\(z\\) to \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\).</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\eta\\le\\sigma&lt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\), sample independent standard Gaussians\n\\[\nc,g\\in\\mathbb R^X,\\qquad \\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal C},\n\\qquad P=P_{c+\\sigma g,\\eta\\lambda}.\n\\]\nDefine tests, each failing when its two answers differ:</p><p class=\"li\">\\((\\mathrm T1)\\): compare \\(f(P_{c,0}(B))\\) with \\(f(P(B))\\), with budget \\(b_1=10\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\((\\mathrm T2)\\): independently resample each row with probability \\(\\delta^2/m\\), obtaining \\(B'\\); compare \\(f(P(B))\\) with \\(f(P(B'))\\), using the same table \\(P\\), with budget \\(b_2=10(\\delta+\\eta)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\((\\mathrm T3)\\): choose uniform \\(z\\in\\mathcal C\\); compare \\(f(P^{z,+}(B))\\) with \\(f(P^{z,-}(B))\\), with budget \\(b_3=|\\mathcal C|^{-1}\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) be the failure probability of test \\(j\\).</p><p>For rational \\(J\\ge1\\), fix, before choosing \\(m,n\\),\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\frac1{2560J},\n\\qquad\n\\delta=\\eta=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2560J},\n\\qquad\nR=\\lceil\\delta^{-2}\\rceil,\n\\qquad\nT=\\left\\lceil\\frac{32J}{\\eta\\sigma^4}\\right\\rceil.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nChoose an integer \\(s\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\ns+2&gt;\\sigma^{-2},\n\\qquad\n(s+2)(1+\\sigma^2)^{-(s+2)}\\le\\frac1{16}.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nF_B(c)=\\mathbb E_{g,\\lambda}f(P_{c+\\sigma g,\\eta\\lambda}(B)),\n\\qquad\nh_x(B,c)=\\sqrt N\\,\\partial_{c_x}F_B(c).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nFor arrays on \\(X\\), use\n\\[\n\\langle v,w\\rangle_x=\\frac1N\\sum_xv_xw_x,\n\\qquad\n\\|v\\|_{2,x}^2=\\frac1N\\sum_x|v_x|^2.\n\\]\nFourier row degree counts the number of rows with nonzero frequencies, not their vector-space dimensions. Let \\(\\Pi_B\\) project onto row degree at most \\(Rm\\), and let \\(\\Pi_c\\) project onto total Hermite degree at most \\(s\\). Set\n\\[\nL=\\Pi_B\\Pi_c h.\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nFor an array \\(v\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathfrak d_X(v)\n=\n\\max_{z\\in\\mathcal C}\n\\left|\\frac1N\\sum_xv_x(-1)^{z_x}\\right|\n=\n\\max_{z^1,\\ldots,z^m}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_xv_x\\prod_{i=1}^m(-1)^{z^i_{x_{-i}}}\n\\right|.\n\\tag{3.17}\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\varepsilon_j\\le Jb_j\\) for \\(j=1,2,3\\), then, for every \\(m,n\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\langle L,h\\rangle_x\n=\n\\mathbb E\\|L\\|_{2,x}^2\n\\ge\\frac12,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\|L\\|_{2,x}^2\\le\\sigma^{-2},\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L)\\ge\\frac1{4T}.\n\\tag{3.18}\n\\]\nThe field \\(L\\) has row degree at most \\(Rm\\) and Gaussian degree at most \\(s\\). All constants preceding \\(m,n\\) are effective and independent of \\(m,n\\), the affine alphabets, and the weighted proof family.</p>", "proof": "<p>The choices (3.7) are effective rational choices. An integer \\(s\\) satisfying (3.8) is found by increasing integers and testing rational inequalities, using the decay established in Lemma \\(3.2\\). The degree and energy assertions follow from that lemma. It remains to prove (3.18).</p><p>For fixed \\(B\\), define\n\\[\n\\Phi_B(t)=f\\bigl(a\\mapsto\\operatorname{sgn}(t_{B(a)})\\bigr),\n\\qquad\n\\Psi_B(t)=\\mathbb E_\\lambda\\Phi_B(t+\\eta\\lambda).\n\\]\nGaussian differentiation in the truth-table coordinates gives\n\\[\n\\partial_{t_z}\\Psi_B(t)\n=\n\\eta^{-1}\\mathbb E_\\lambda\\lambda_z\\Phi_B(t+\\eta\\lambda).\n\\]\nThe input corresponding to \\(F_B\\) is\n\\[\nt_z=N^{-1/2}\\sum_x(c_x+\\sigma g_x)(-1)^{z_x}.\n\\]\nThe chain rule therefore gives\n\\[\nh_x(B,c)=\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal C}\\mu_z(B,c)(-1)^{z_x},\n\\qquad\n\\mu_z(B,c)\n=\n\\eta^{-1}\\mathbb E_{g,\\lambda}\\lambda_zf(P(B)).\n\\tag{3.19}\n\\]\nThere are finitely many table coordinates, and\n\\[\n|\\partial_{t_z}\\Psi_B|\\le\\eta^{-1}\\mathbb E|\\lambda_z|\n\\]\nuniformly, so differentiation passes through the \\(g\\)-average.</p><p>Condition on \\(\\xi,B,c,g\\) and every coordinate of \\(\\lambda\\) except \\(\\lambda_z\\). Put\n\\[\nf_+=f(P^{z,+}(B)),\n\\qquad\nf_-=f(P^{z,-}(B)).\n\\]\nThese do not depend on \\(\\lambda_z\\). With\n\\[\n\\phi(u)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-u^2/2},\n\\]\none-dimensional Gaussian integration gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\lambda_z}\\lambda_zf(P(B))\n=\nf_+\\int_{-t_z/\\eta}^{\\infty}u\\phi(u)\\,du\n+\nf_-\\int_{-\\infty}^{-t_z/\\eta}u\\phi(u)\\,du\n=\n(f_+-f_-)\\phi(t_z/\\eta).\n\\]\nIts absolute value is at most \\(2\\mathbf1_{\\{f_+\\ne f_-\\}}\\). No injectivity of \\(B:A\\to\\mathcal C\\) is required.</p><p>Let \\(W=\\sum_z|\\mu_z|\\). Taking absolute values after the remaining averages and summing gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EW\n\\le\n\\frac2\\eta\\sum_z\n\\mathbb P[f(P^{z,+}(B))\\ne f(P^{z,-}(B))]\n=\n\\frac{2|\\mathcal C|}{\\eta}\\varepsilon_3\n\\le\\frac{2J}{\\eta}.\n\\tag{3.20}\n\\]\nThus \\(h\\) belongs to \\(W\\) times the convex hull of the signed codeword arrays, and\n\\[\n|\\langle L,h\\rangle_x|\n\\le W\\mathfrak d_X(L).\n\\tag{3.21}\n\\]</p><p>The contribution of \\(W&gt;T\\) is bounded by\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[\\mathbf1_{\\{W&gt;T\\}}|\\langle L,h\\rangle_x|\\bigr]\n&amp;\\le\n\\sigma^{-1}\n(\\mathbb E\\|L\\|_{2,x}^2)^{1/2}\n\\mathbb P[W&gt;T]^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sigma^{-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2J}{\\eta T}}\n\\le\\frac14.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbb E\\langle L,h\\rangle_x\\ge1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac14\n\\le\n\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_{\\{W\\le T\\}}\\langle L,h\\rangle_x]\n\\le\nT\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L).\n\\]\nThis proves (3.18). All bounds are averaged over the weighted proof family; no individual-index test bound was assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Honest proof budgets.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the tests of Proposition \\(3\\), but allow any \\(m,n\\ge2\\), \\(0<\\eta\\le\\sigma<1\\), and \\(0<\\delta<1\\), without imposing (3.7)–(3.8).", "statement": "<p>Use the tests of Proposition \\(3\\), but allow any \\(m,n\\ge2\\), \\(0&lt;\\eta\\le\\sigma&lt;1\\), and \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\), without imposing (3.7)–(3.8).</p><p>For any labels \\(a_\\xi\\in A_\\xi\\), the evaluation proofs \\(f_\\xi(P)=P(a_\\xi)\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_1\\le2\\sigma,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_2\\le2\\delta+2\\eta,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_3=|\\mathcal C|^{-1}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\varepsilon_j\\le b_j\\) for \\(j=1,2,3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on \\(\\xi\\) and its label \\(a\\). For \\(z\\in\\mathcal C\\), put\n\\[\nv_z=N^{-1/2}\\bigl((-1)^{z_x}\\bigr)_{x\\in X},\n\\qquad \\|v_z\\|=1.\n\\]</p><p>For the first test, conditional on \\(B\\), the input \\(\\langle c,v_{B(a)}\\rangle\\) is standard Gaussian. Its added perturbation\n\\[\n\\sigma\\langle g,v_{B(a)}\\rangle+\\eta\\lambda_{B(a)}\n\\]\nis independent Gaussian with standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+\\eta^2}\\). The sign identity gives disagreement probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac1\\pi\\arctan\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+\\eta^2}\n\\le\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+\\eta^2}\\le2\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>For the second test, let \\(D\\) be the relative Hamming distance between \\(B(a)\\) and \\(B'(a)\\). At a point \\(x\\), the evaluation can change only if at least one of its \\(m\\) rows was selected. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb ED\\le\\delta^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\|v_{B(a)}-v_{B'(a)}\\|\n=\n2\\mathbb E\\sqrt D\\le2\\delta.\n\\]\nConditional on \\(B,B'\\), first omit the table perturbations and apply the sign identity to the isotropic Gaussian \\(c+\\sigma g\\). Restoring each perturbation changes its sign with probability at most\n\\[\n\\eta/\\sqrt{1+\\sigma^2}\\le\\eta.\n\\]\nA union bound gives \\(\\varepsilon_2\\le2\\delta+2\\eta\\). Independence between the two table perturbations is unnecessary, so this covers \\(B(a)=B'(a)\\).</p><p>For the third test, the evaluation answers differ exactly when \\(z=B(a)\\), giving probability \\(|\\mathcal C|^{-1}\\).</p><p>All bounds hold conditional on the selected label and may be averaged over \\(\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Gradient energy and the two cutoffs.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the setting and parameter choices of Proposition \\(3\\), assume only \\( \\varepsilon_1\\le Jb_1,\\qquad \\varepsilon_2\\le Jb_2. \\) Then \\( \\mathbb E\\|h\\|_{2,x}^2\\ge\\frac34, \\) …", "statement": "<p>Under the setting and parameter choices of Proposition \\(3\\), assume only\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_1\\le Jb_1,\\qquad \\varepsilon_2\\le Jb_2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|h\\|_{2,x}^2\\ge\\frac34,\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|(I-\\Pi_c)h\\|_{2,x}^2\\le\\frac1{16},\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|(I-\\Pi_B)h\\|_{2,x}^2\\le\\frac1{32},\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nand (3.14) holds.</p><p>For every folded proof, without any test assumption,\n\\[\n\\|h(B,c)\\|_{2,x}\\le\\sigma^{-1},\n\\qquad\n|h_x(B,c)|\\le\\frac{\\sqrt N}{\\sigma}.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nQ_B(u)=\\mathbb E_\\lambda f(P_{u,\\eta\\lambda}(B)).\n\\]\nThis is bounded and measurable: it is obtained from finitely many threshold signs followed by a function on a finite set. Gaussian convolution and (2.3) give\n\\[\nh_x(B,c)\n=\n\\frac{\\sqrt N}{\\sigma}\n\\mathbb E_{g,\\lambda}g_xf(P(B)).\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\xi,B,c\\), the functions \\(g_x\\) are orthonormal in the joint probability space of \\((g,\\lambda)\\). Bessel’s inequality yields\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_{2,x}^2\n=\n\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\\sum_x\n\\left|\\mathbb E_{g,\\lambda}g_xf(P(B))\\right|^2\n\\le\\sigma^{-2}.\n\\]\nThe individual-coordinate bound follows as well.</p><p>Negating \\(u\\) and replacing \\(\\lambda\\) by \\(-\\lambda\\) negates every table entry almost surely. Each entry has a continuous perturbation of positive variance. Folding gives \\(Q_B(-u)=-Q_B(u)\\), hence \\(F_B(-c)=-F_B(c)\\) and \\(\\mathbb E_cF_B(c)=0\\).</p><p>Let \\(f_0(B,c)=f(P_{c,0}(B))\\), so \\(|f_0|=1\\). Jensen’s inequality and the first test give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|F_B(c)-f_0(B,c)|^2\n\\le4\\varepsilon_1\n\\le40J\\sigma\n\\le\\frac1{64}.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\|F\\|_2\\ge7/8\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathbb EF^2\\ge49/64&gt;3/4.\n\\]\nThe mean-zero Gaussian energy inequality implies (3.11), since\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|h\\|_{2,x}^2\n=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_x|\\partial_{c_x}F_B(c)|^2\n\\ge\\mathbb EF_B(c)^2.\n\\]</p><p>For the Gaussian cutoff, put\n\\[\n\\rho=(1+\\sigma^2)^{-1/2},\n\\qquad\nG_B(v)=Q_B(\\sqrt{1+\\sigma^2}\\,v).\n\\]\nThen \\(F_B=T_\\rho G_B\\) and \\(|G_B|\\le1\\). If\n\\[\nG_B=\\sum_I a_I(B)\\psi_I,\n\\]\nthen \\(F_B\\) has coefficients \\(\\rho^{|I|}a_I(B)\\). Gaussian integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\int\\partial_{c_x}F_B(c)\\psi_J(c)\\,d\\gamma_N(c)\n=\n\\sqrt{J_x+1}\n\\int F_B(c)\\psi_{J+e_x}(c)\\,d\\gamma_N(c).\n\\]\nThe integration by parts is legitimate because \\(F_B\\) and its first derivatives are bounded and Gaussian-weighted polynomials have integrable tails. This identifies the actual derivative coefficients with the formally differentiated expansion.</p><p>The choice (3.8) implies\n\\[\n\\sup_{k\\ge s+2}k(1+\\sigma^2)^{-k}\\le\\frac1{16};\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nindeed, the ratio of successive terms is\n\\[\n\\frac{1+1/k}{1+\\sigma^2}&lt;1\n\\]\nwhen \\(k&gt;\\sigma^{-2}\\), and the sequence tends to zero. Consequently, Parseval gives, for every \\(\\xi,B\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_c\\|(I-\\Pi_c)h\\|_{2,x}^2\n=\n\\sum_{|I|\\ge s+2}|I|\\rho^{2|I|}|a_I(B)|^2\n\\le\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nThe shift to \\(s+2\\) occurs because differentiation lowers degree by one.</p><p>For the row cutoff, use the same \\(g,\\lambda\\) in the two representations (3.15). Bessel’s inequality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|h(B,c)-h(B',c)\\|_{2,x}^2\n\\le\n\\sigma^{-2}\\mathbb E|f(P(B))-f(P(B'))|^2\n=\n4\\varepsilon_2/\\sigma^2\n\\le\\frac1{32}.\n\\tag{3.16}\n\\]\nWrite \\(a=\\delta^2/m\\). The row-resampling channel has eigenvalue \\((1-a)^k\\) on row degree \\(k\\). Stationarity and Parseval show that a degree-\\(k\\) squared Fourier norm contributes\n\\[\n2\\bigl(1-(1-a)^k\\bigr)\n\\]\ntimes that norm to the left side of (3.16). If \\(k&gt;Rm\\), then \\(ka&gt;R\\delta^2\\ge1\\). The binomial inequality gives\n\\[\n(1-a)^k\\le(1+ka)^{-1}&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nThe displayed multiplier therefore exceeds one, proving (3.13).</p><p>Finally, the commuting orthogonal projections give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\langle L,h\\rangle_x=\\mathbb E\\|L\\|_{2,x}^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|h-L\\|_{2,x}^2\\le\\frac1{32}+\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nTogether with (3.11), this yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|L\\|_{2,x}^2\\ge21/32&gt;1/2.\n\\]\nProjection and (3.6) give the upper bound \\(\\sigma^{-2}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 3.2, “Inner decoding.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the folded proofs, face arrays, tests, and smoothed field \\(h\\) defined in Proposition \\(3\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the folded proofs, face arrays, tests, and smoothed field \\(h\\) defined in Proposition \\(3\\).</p><p>For every rational \\(J\\ge1\\), there are effectively selectable integers \\(m,n\\ge2\\) and positive rational constants\n\\[\n\\sigma,\\delta,\\eta,p,\\theta&lt;1,\n\\qquad \\eta\\le\\sigma,\n\\]\ndepending only on \\(J\\), such that the following holds for every finite weighted family of nonempty affine alphabets and folded bit proofs.</p><p>If\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_j\\le Jb_j\n\\qquad(j=1,2,3),\n\\]\ndraw \\(\\xi\\), a standard Gaussian \\(c\\in\\mathbb R^X\\), independent uniform \\(x\\in X\\) and \\(i\\in[m]\\), and all rows other than \\(B_{x_{-i}}^i\\) independently and uniformly in \\(D(A_\\xi)\\). Then, with probability at least \\(p\\), the function\n\\[\nb\\longmapsto h_x(B[B_{x_{-i}}^i\\leftarrow b],c)\n\\]\nhas an odd Fourier coefficient of absolute value at least \\(\\theta\\). Equivalently,\n\\[\n\\max_{\\alpha\\in D(A_\\xi)^*:\\,\\alpha(1)=1}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{b\\in D(A_\\xi)}\nh_x(B[B_{x_{-i}}^i\\leftarrow b],c)(-1)^{\\alpha(b)}\n\\right|\n\\ge\\theta.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]</p><p>Without any test assumption, the pointwise bounds (3.6) hold.</p><p>For the lemmas below, first fix the smoothing constants \\(R,s,T,\\sigma,\\delta,\\eta\\) from Proposition \\(3\\), independently of \\(m,n\\) and the alphabet. A row-frequency family is\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol\\alpha=(\\alpha_r:r\\in\\mathcal R),\n\\qquad\n\\chi_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}(B)=\\prod_{r\\in\\mathcal R}\\chi_{\\alpha_r}(B_r).\n\\]\nIts exact row mask is\n\\[\nS_i=\\{y:\\alpha_y^i\\ne0\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\sum_i|S_i|.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\xi\\),\n\\[\nL_x(B,c)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{|I|\\le s\\\\|S(\\boldsymbol\\alpha)|\\le Rm}}\n\\widehat h_x(\\boldsymbol\\alpha,I)\n\\chi_{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}(B)\\psi_I(c).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nA “complete exact-mask/Hermite component” includes every frequency family with the specified mask \\(S\\) and Hermite index \\(I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the smoothing parameters by Proposition \\(3\\). Choose \\(m\\) satisfying (4.4), set \\(d_0\\) as in (4.6), and put \\(e=d_0/10\\). Make these successive choices, with strict slack:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Choose a positive integer \\(H\\) with \\(\\sigma^{-2}/H&lt;e\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Choose positive rational \\(\\gamma\\) with \\(H\\gamma&lt;e^2/4\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Choose a positive integer \\(A_0\\) so large that\n   \\[\n   2\\sigma^{-2}/A_0&lt;e,\n   \\qquad\n   H^3/A_0&lt;e^2/4.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(\\sqrt{H(\\gamma+H^2/A_0)}&lt;e\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Choose an integer \\(\\ell\\ge2\\) so large that\n   \\[\n   2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m}&lt;e.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Choose an integer \\(n&gt;\\ell\\) so large that\n   \\[\n   \\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{(s+Rm)\\ell/(n-1)}&lt;e.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. Now \\(N=n^m\\), \\(M=mn^{m-1}\\), and\n   \\[\n   C_0=4^M\\binom{N+s}{s}\n   \\]\n   are fixed. Choose positive rationals \\(p,\\theta&lt;1\\) so small that\n   \\[\n   \\theta^2+\\frac{mpN}{\\sigma^2}\n   &lt;\n   \\frac{e^4\\sigma^4\\gamma^2}{C_0^2}.\n   \\]\n   This makes \\(e_{\\mathrm{atom}}&lt;e\\).</p><p>All choices are finite and use explicit inequalities. Roots can be removed by raising positive quantities to integer powers, so rational arithmetic and integer search suffice. Neither the alphabet size nor the weighted proof distribution enters the choices.</p><p>If the row-atom conclusion failed, Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.6\\) would apply. Every pair-class correlation in (4.28) would be less than \\(6e&lt;d_0\\), and the class-\\(0\\) correlation in (4.29) would be less than \\(3e&lt;d_0\\). Summing over all \\(1+\\binom m2\\) classes would give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L)\n&lt;\n(1+\\tbinom m2)d_0\n=\n\\frac1{4T},\n\\]\ncontradicting (4.7). This proves the probability at least \\(p\\) in (3.5). The unconditional pointwise bounds are those of Lemma \\(3.2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the test bounds in Proposition \\(4\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume the test bounds in Proposition \\(4\\). Choose \\(m&gt;s+1\\) so that\n\\[\n\\binom{m-s}{2}&gt;Rm.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThen the terms of \\(L_x\\) can be partitioned, using only \\(x,I,S\\), into classes indexed by\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{(i,j):1\\le i&lt;j\\le m\\}.\n\\]\nClass \\(0\\) consists of the terms with \\(I_x&gt;0\\). Every nonzero term in class \\((i,j)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\alpha_{x_{-i}}^i=\\alpha_{x_{-j}}^j,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_{x_{-i}}^i(1)=1.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nWriting \\(L^a\\) for the class arrays and\n\\[\nd_0=\\frac{1}{4T(1+\\binom m2)},\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\frac1{4T}\n\\le\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L)\n\\le\n\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal K}\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L^a).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First fix distinct axes \\(i,j\\), a tuple\n\\[\nq\\in[n]^{[m]\\setminus\\{i,j\\}},\n\\]\nand \\(a\\in D(A)\\). Add \\(a\\) to every row \\(B_y^i\\) with \\(y_{-j}=q\\) and every row \\(B_y^j\\) with \\(y_{-i}=q\\). At a point \\(u\\), either both additions occur or neither occurs, so the value \\(B(u)\\) is unchanged. Hence \\(h_x\\) is unchanged. Orthogonality implies that every nonzero Fourier–Hermite coefficient satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{y:y_{-j}=q}\\alpha_y^i\n+\n\\sum_{y:y_{-i}=q}\\alpha_y^j\n=0\n\\quad\\text{in }D(A)^*.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p><p>For a second symmetry, fix \\(r=(i,v)\\) and let\n\\[\nk_u=\\mathbf1_{\\{u_{-i}=v\\}}.\n\\]\nThis is a codeword. Write \\(B+1_r\\) for addition of the constant-one function in row \\(r\\), and\n\\[\nD_kc=\\bigl((-1)^{k_u}c_u\\bigr)_u.\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\nP_{c+\\sigma g,\\eta\\lambda}(z+k)\n=\nP_{D_kc+\\sigma D_kg,\\eta\\lambda'}(z),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda'_z=\\lambda_{z+k},\n\\]\nand Gaussian invariance give\n\\[\nF_{B+1_r}(c)=F_B(D_kc),\n\\qquad\nh_x(B+1_r,c)=(-1)^{k_x}h_x(B,D_kc).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\psi_I(D_kc)=(-1)^{\\sum_uk_uI_u}\\psi_I(c),\n\\]\nevery nonzero coefficient satisfies\n\\[\n\\alpha_r(1)\n\\equiv\nk_x+\\sum_uk_uI_u\n\\pmod2.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>Assign terms with \\(I_x&gt;0\\) to class \\(0\\). For each remaining \\((I,S)\\), choose \\(i&lt;j\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. No \\(u\\) with \\(I_u&gt;0\\) satisfies \\(u_{-i}=x_{-i}\\) or \\(u_{-j}=x_{-j}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Among rows of \\(S_i\\) above \\(x_{-ij}\\), only \\(x_{-i}\\) is permitted; among rows of \\(S_j\\) above \\(x_{-ij}\\), only \\(x_{-j}\\) is permitted.</p><p>Because \\(I_x=0\\), a positive Hermite support point agrees with \\(x\\) after deleting at most one axis. Thus at most \\(s\\) axes are forbidden by the first condition.</p><p>A nonlocal row \\(y\\in S_i\\), \\(y\\ne x_{-i}\\), obstructs the second condition for at most one other axis \\(j\\): it must differ from \\(x_{-i}\\) only at \\(j\\). The masks therefore forbid at most \\(Rm\\) unordered pairs. Inequality (4.4) leaves an admissible pair.</p><p>Fix an ordering of the pairs and choose the first admissible one. The rule uses only \\(x,I,S\\). For a nonzero term in class \\((i,j)\\), apply (4.2) at \\(q=x_{-ij}\\). Only the two local frequencies remain, so they are equal. Apply (4.3) to \\((i,x_{-i})\\); its Hermite sum vanishes by the first condition and \\(k_x=1\\). This proves (4.5).</p><p>Finally, subadditivity of \\(\\mathfrak d_X\\) and Proposition \\(3\\) give (4.7).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Retain the class partition of Lemma \\(4.1\\).", "statement": "<p>Retain the class partition of Lemma \\(4.1\\). Let \\(2\\le\\ell&lt;n\\). Independently choose a uniform \\(\\ell\\)-element subset \\(Y_i\\subseteq[n]\\) for each axis, and put\n\\[\nY=\\prod_{i=1}^mY_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\in Y\\), retain from \\(L_x^a\\) only terms whose positive Hermite support on \\(Y\\) is contained in \\(\\{x\\}\\) and whose masked rows on the faces of \\(Y\\) are contained in\n\\[\n\\{(r,x_{-r}):r\\in[m]\\}.\n\\]\nWrite the retained field as\n\\[\nv_x^a=Q_x^{a,Y}h_x.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\mathfrak d_X(v)\\le\\mathbb E_Y\\mathfrak d_Y(v|_Y)\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nfor every deterministic array \\(v\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}|L_x^a-v_x^a|^2\n\\le\n\\sigma^{-2}(s+Rm)\\frac{\\ell}{n-1},\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}|v_x^a|^2\\le\\sigma^{-2}.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nThus the expected normalized \\(L^1\\) localization loss is at most\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{loc}}\n=\n\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{(s+Rm)\\ell/(n-1)}.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]</p><p>For class \\(0\\), conditional on \\(\\xi,Y\\), all rows \\(B\\), and all Gaussians outside \\(Y\\), each \\(v_x^0\\) is a function of \\(c_x\\) alone and has conditional mean zero.</p><p>For class \\((i,j)\\), let \\(\\mathcal F_{ij,Y}\\) consist of \\(\\xi,Y\\), all Gaussians, all tables other than \\(B^i,B^j\\), and the rows of those two tables outside the faces of \\(Y\\). Conditional on this background,\n\\[\nv_x^{ij}\n=\nV_x(B_{x_{-i}}^i+B_{x_{-j}}^j),\n\\qquad\nV_x(b)=\\sum_{\\alpha:\\alpha(1)=1}\\widehat V_x(\\alpha)\\chi_\\alpha(b),\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nwhere \\(V_x\\) is independent of every remaining on-subbox row in tables \\(i,j\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}\\|V_x\\|_2^2\\le\\sigma^{-2}.\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose face signs attaining \\(\\mathfrak d_X(v)\\), restrict them to \\(Y\\), and use that a uniform point of a random \\(Y\\) has uniform marginal on \\(X\\). The original correlation is the average of the restricted correlations. Taking absolute values proves (4.8), even when the maximizing signs depend on the full array.</p><p>Conditional on a uniform point of \\(Y\\) being \\(x\\), the subsets \\(Y_i\\) are independent uniform \\(\\ell\\)-subsets containing \\(x_i\\). For a fixed \\(u\\ne x\\), membership in \\(Y\\) requires selection of at least one prescribed value other than \\(x_i\\), and has probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell-1}{n-1}.\n\\]\nThe same holds for a face tuple different from the corresponding face of \\(x\\). A term has at most \\(s\\) positive Hermite positions and \\(Rm\\) masked rows. A union bound and orthogonality give (4.9). Each retained projection contracts squared norm; averaging and (3.6) give (4.10). Cauchy–Schwarz gives (4.11).</p><p>For class \\(0\\), every retained term has \\(I_x&gt;0\\) and no other positive Hermite index on \\(Y\\), proving the asserted centered one-coordinate description.</p><p>For class \\((i,j)\\), localization leaves no other on-subbox row in either selected table. Equation (4.5) makes the local factors equal odd characters, whose product is the character of their sum. This gives (4.12). Integrating the two independent local rows makes their sum uniform, so (4.10) gives (4.13).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Uniform transfer through the selected projection.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(4\\), for \\(r=(i,x_{-i})\\), define \\( a_{x,i}(B_{\\ne r},c) = \\max_{\\alpha:\\alpha(1)=1} \\left| \\mathbb E_{b\\in D(A)} h_x(B[r\\leftarrow b],c)\\chi_\\alpha(b) \\right|. \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(4\\), for \\(r=(i,x_{-i})\\), define\n\\[\na_{x,i}(B_{\\ne r},c)\n=\n\\max_{\\alpha:\\alpha(1)=1}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{b\\in D(A)}\nh_x(B[r\\leftarrow b],c)\\chi_\\alpha(b)\n\\right|.\n\\]\nThen \\(a_{x,i}\\le\\sqrt N/\\sigma\\).</p><p>Let \\(Q_x^Y\\) select any collection of complete exact-mask/Hermite components with \\(|I|\\le s\\), using only \\(x,Y,I,S\\). For a fixed axis \\(i\\), let \\(q_{x,i}^Y\\) be the maximum odd row coefficient of \\(Q_x^Yh_x\\) in row \\((i,x_{-i})\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}q_{x,i}^Y\n\\le\nC_0\n\\left(\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in X}a_{x,i}^2\\right)^{1/2},\n\\qquad\nC_0=4^M\\binom{N+s}{s}.\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nThis holds for every weighted distribution of proof indices.</p><p>If the conclusion of Proposition \\(4\\) fails for positive \\(p,\\theta\\), meaning\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{\\xi,x,i,B_{\\ne(i,x_{-i})},c}[a_{x,i}\\ge\\theta]&lt;p,\n\\]\nthen, for each fixed \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in X}a_{x,i}^2\n\\le D,\n\\qquad\nD=\\theta^2+\\frac{mpN}{\\sigma^2}.\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]\nFor a retained pair slice \\(V_x\\), put\n\\[\nq_x=\\max_{\\alpha:\\alpha(1)=1}|\\widehat V_x(\\alpha)|.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}q_x\\le C_0\\sqrt D,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb P[q_x&gt;\\gamma]\\le\\frac{C_0\\sqrt D}{\\gamma}\n\\tag{4.16}\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), with probability over a uniform point of \\(Y\\) and its slice background.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a row \\(t\\), let \\(E_t\\) average that row uniformly. The exact-mask projection is\n\\[\nP_S=\\prod_{t\\in S}(\\mathrm{Id}-E_t)\\prod_{t\\notin S}E_t.\n\\]\nThe Hermite-index projection is\n\\[\nH_Iu(B,c)=\\psi_I(c)\\mathbb E_{c'}\\psi_I(c')u(B,c').\n\\]\nThere are \\(2^M\\) masks and \\(\\binom{N+s}{s}\\) indices of degree at most \\(s\\). Thus \\(Q_x^Y\\) is a sum of at most their product of operators \\(P_SH_I\\).</p><p>Taking a Fourier coefficient in the distinguished row commutes with all other row averages. If \\(r\\notin S\\), \\(E_r\\) annihilates every odd coefficient. If \\(r\\in S\\), \\(\\mathrm{Id}-E_r\\) leaves it unchanged. Expand all remaining factors \\(\\mathrm{Id}-E_t\\) into signed averages, producing at most \\(2^M\\) terms.</p><p>For one such term, its maximum odd coefficient is at most\n\\[\n|\\psi_I(c)|\n\\mathbb E_{c',\\,\\mathrm{averaged\\ rows}}\n|\\psi_I(c')|a_{x,i}(B'_{\\ne r},c').\n\\]\nThe maximum is moved inside the absolute-value integral; no sum over row frequencies is introduced.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\xi,x\\), average the output \\(c\\) and all other output rows. Uniform row averaging preserves product measure,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|\\psi_I(c)|\\le1,\n\\]\nand Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|\\psi_I(c')|a_{x,i}(B_{\\ne r},c')\n\\le\n(\\mathbb Ea_{x,i}^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nSumming the at most \\(4^M\\binom{N+s}{s}\\) expanded terms and then applying Cauchy–Schwarz over \\(\\xi,Y,x\\in Y\\) proves (4.14), using the uniform marginal of \\(x\\).</p><p>If the row-atom conclusion fails, its event has probability less than \\(mp\\) for every fixed axis. The pointwise bound \\(a_{x,i}\\le\\sqrt N/\\sigma\\) gives (4.15).</p><p>For a pair class, the coefficient of \\(V_x(B_{x_{-i}}^i+b')\\) in the first local row equals\n\\[\n\\widehat V_x(\\alpha)\\chi_\\alpha(b').\n\\]\nIts maximum magnitude is \\(q_x\\), independently of \\(b'\\). Apply (4.14), (4.15), and Markov’s inequality to obtain (4.16).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the failed row-atom assertion and notation of Lemma \\(4.3\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume the failed row-atom assertion and notation of Lemma \\(4.3\\). Let \\(A_0,H,\\gamma&gt;0\\), and define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{clip}_{A_0}(z)=\\max(-A_0,\\min(z,A_0)).\n\\]\nThere are processed fields \\(w^0,w^{ij}\\) on \\(Y\\) with the following properties.</p><p>For class \\(0\\),\n\\[\n|w_x^0|\\le2A_0,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}|v_x^0-w_x^0|\n\\le\\frac{2\\sigma^{-2}}{A_0}.\n\\tag{4.18}\n\\]\nConditional on the class-\\(0\\) background of Lemma \\(4.2\\), the \\(w_x^0\\) are independent centered functions of the distinct Gaussians \\(c_x\\).</p><p>For class \\((i,j)\\),\n\\[\nw_x^{ij}=W_x(B_{x_{-i}}^i+B_{x_{-j}}^j),\n\\]\nwhere \\(W_x\\) is measurable with respect to \\(\\mathcal F_{ij,Y}\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}|v_x^{ij}-w_x^{ij}|\n\\le\n\\frac{\\sigma^{-2}}H+e_{\\mathrm{atom}}+\\frac{\\sigma^{-2}}{A_0},\n\\tag{4.20}\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{atom}}\n=\n\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{C_0/\\gamma}\\,D^{1/4}.\n\\tag{4.19}\n\\]\nEvery processed slice satisfies\n\\[\n|W_x|\\le A_0,\\qquad\n\\|W_x\\|_2\\le H,\\qquad\n\\max_\\alpha|\\widehat W_x(\\alpha)|\n\\le\\varepsilon_0,\n\\quad\n\\varepsilon_0=\\gamma+\\frac{H^2}{A_0}.\n\\tag{4.21}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use\n\\[\n|z-\\operatorname{clip}_{A_0}(z)|\\le z^2/A_0.\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]</p><p>For class \\(0\\), set\n\\[\nw_x^0\n=\n\\operatorname{clip}_{A_0}(v_x^0)\n-\n\\mathbb E_{c_x}\\operatorname{clip}_{A_0}(v_x^0),\n\\]\nwith all rows and Gaussians outside \\(Y\\) fixed. Since \\(\\mathbb E_{c_x}v_x^0=0\\), (4.17) bounds both the clipping loss and the subtracted mean. Equation (4.10) proves (4.18). The conditional independence and centering follow from the description in Lemma \\(4.2\\).</p><p>For a pair class, process the entire slice \\(V_x\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Set it to zero if \\(\\|V_x\\|_2&gt;H\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Set it to zero if \\(q_x&gt;\\gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Clip every value of a surviving slice to \\([-A_0,A_0]\\).</p><p>Both discard decisions depend only on the slice background, independently of the sampled local rows. Put \\(\\nu_x=\\|V_x\\|_2\\). The first discard costs at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}\\nu_x\\mathbf1_{\\{\\nu_x&gt;H\\}}\n\\le\nH^{-1}\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}\\nu_x^2\n\\le\\sigma^{-2}/H.\n\\]\nFor the second discard, Cauchy–Schwarz, (4.13), and (4.16) give a loss at most\n\\[\n\\sigma^{-1}\n\\left(\\frac{C_0\\sqrt D}{\\gamma}\\right)^{1/2}\n=e_{\\mathrm{atom}}.\n\\]\nThe clipping loss is at most \\(\\sigma^{-2}/A_0\\), proving (4.20).</p><p>A surviving slice has conditional \\(L^1\\) clipping error at most \\(H^2/A_0\\). Every Fourier coefficient changes by at most that amount. Before clipping, its spectrum is odd and every coefficient has magnitude at most \\(\\gamma\\). Thus (4.21) holds for all frequencies, including for discarded slices. Symmetric clipping also preserves oddness, because \\(V_x(b+1)=-V_x(b)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, “Box Cauchy–Schwarz.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite product \\(Y=\\prod_{j=1}^mY_j\\) and a real array \\(v:Y\\to\\mathbb R\\), choose independent uniform \\(u^0,u^1\\in Y\\) and put \\( u^\\omega=(u_1^{\\omega_1},\\ldots,u_m^{\\omega_m}) \\qquad(\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^m). \\) …", "statement": "<p>For a finite product \\(Y=\\prod_{j=1}^mY_j\\) and a real array \\(v:Y\\to\\mathbb R\\), choose independent uniform \\(u^0,u^1\\in Y\\) and put\n\\[\nu^\\omega=(u_1^{\\omega_1},\\ldots,u_m^{\\omega_m})\n\\qquad(\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^m).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathfrak d_Y(v)^{2^m}\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{u^0,u^1}\n\\prod_{\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^m}v(u^\\omega).\n\\tag{4.22}\n\\]\nIn particular, the right side is nonnegative.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix face multipliers \\(a_j:Y_{-j}\\to[-1,1]\\). At stage \\(k\\), double the first \\(k\\) coordinates and keep the last \\(m-k\\) coordinates single. For \\(\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^k\\), write\n\\[\nz^\\omega=(u_1^{\\omega_1},\\ldots,u_k^{\\omega_k},u_{k+1},\\ldots,u_m),\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nA_k\n=\n\\mathbb E\n\\left[\n\\prod_{\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^k}v(z^\\omega)\n\\prod_{j=k+1}^m\n\\prod_{\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^k}\na_j(z^\\omega_{-j})\n\\right].\n\\]\nThus \\(A_0\\) is the original correlation and \\(A_m\\) is the right side of (4.22).</p><p>In \\(A_{k-1}\\), the product of factors \\(a_k\\) is independent of the single coordinate \\(u_k\\) and has absolute value at most one. Put the \\(u_k\\)-average innermost and apply Cauchy–Schwarz over the outer variables, removing that product. The result is the average of the square of the remaining inner average. Expanding with two independent values \\(u_k^0,u_k^1\\) gives \\(A_k\\). Hence\n\\[\n|A_{k-1}|^2\\le A_k\n\\qquad(1\\le k\\le m).\n\\]\nIteration gives \\(|A_0|^{2^m}\\le A_m\\) and nonnegativity of \\(A_m\\). Maximize over the face signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the processed fields of Lemma \\(4.4\\), \\( \\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w^0) \\le 2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m}, \\) and \\( \\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w^{ij}) \\le 2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m} + \\sqrt{H(\\gamma+H^2/A_0)}. \\) Consequently …", "statement": "<p>For the processed fields of Lemma \\(4.4\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w^0)\n\\le\n2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m},\n\\tag{4.24}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w^{ij})\n\\le\n2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m}\n+\n\\sqrt{H(\\gamma+H^2/A_0)}.\n\\tag{4.27}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L^{ij})\n\\le{}&amp;\n\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{(s+Rm)\\ell/(n-1)}\n+\\frac{\\sigma^{-2}}H\\\\\n&amp;+\\sigma^{-1}\\sqrt{C_0/\\gamma}\n\\left(\\theta^2+\\frac{mpN}{\\sigma^2}\\right)^{1/4}\n+\\frac{\\sigma^{-2}}{A_0}\\\\\n&amp;+2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m}\n+\\sqrt{H(\\gamma+H^2/A_0)},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.28)\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_X(L^0)\n\\le\ne_{\\mathrm{loc}}\n+\\frac{2\\sigma^{-2}}{A_0}\n+2A_0(m/\\ell)^{1/2^m}.\n\\tag{4.29}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(Q=2^m\\). Apply Lemma \\(4.5\\) pointwise before averaging randomness. Jensen gives\n\\[\n(\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w))^Q\n\\le\n\\mathbb E\\mathfrak d_Y(w)^Q\n\\le\n\\mathbb E\\mathbb E_{u^0,u^1}\\prod_\\omega w(u^\\omega).\n\\tag{4.23}\n\\]\nThe probability that \\(u_i^0=u_i^1\\) for some \\(i\\) is at most \\(m/\\ell\\). Since all processed fields have absolute bound \\(2A_0\\), these cubes contribute at most\n\\[\n(m/\\ell)(2A_0)^Q\n\\]\nin absolute value.</p><p>On a cube without repetitions, its vertices are distinct. For class \\(0\\), condition on all rows and Gaussians outside \\(Y\\). Its factors are independent centered functions of distinct Gaussian coordinates, so their product has conditional mean zero. This proves (4.24).</p><p>For a pair class, condition on \\(\\mathcal F_{ij,Y}\\) and a cube without repetitions. Partition the vertices into \\(2^{m-2}\\) squares by fixing all coordinates other than \\(i,j\\). The four local sums in a square have the form\n\\[\nr_a+s_b,\\qquad a,b\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_0,r_1,s_0,s_1\\) are independent uniform rows in \\(D(A)\\). For four possibly different slices,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{r_0,r_1,s_0,s_1}\n\\prod_{a,b\\in\\{0,1\\}}W_{ab}(r_a+s_b)\n=\n\\sum_\\alpha\\prod_{a,b\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\widehat W_{ab}(\\alpha).\n\\tag{4.25}\n\\]\nFourier expansion and averaging force all four frequencies to agree. Use the coefficient bound on two factors and Cauchy–Schwarz and Parseval on the other two:\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_\\alpha\\prod_{a,b}\\widehat W_{ab}(\\alpha)\\right|\n\\le\n\\varepsilon_0^2\n\\sum_\\alpha|\\widehat W_{10}(\\alpha)\\widehat W_{11}(\\alpha)|\n\\le\\varepsilon_0^2H^2.\n\\tag{4.26}\n\\]</p><p>Different squares use disjoint rows in both tables, since changing any other coordinate changes a retained coordinate of every row. Their products are conditionally independent, even though their slice functions share the background. The full cube product has absolute conditional expectation at most\n\\[\n(\\varepsilon_0^2H^2)^{2^{m-2}}.\n\\]\nCombine this with the collision bound and (4.23), using\n\\[\n(a+b)^{1/Q}\\le a^{1/Q}+b^{1/Q},\n\\]\nto obtain (4.27).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n|\\mathfrak d_Y(u)-\\mathfrak d_Y(v)|\n\\le\\mathbb E_{x\\in Y}|u_x-v_x|.\n\\]\nCombine (4.8), the localization loss, the processing losses, and the two bounds just proved. This gives (4.28) and (4.29). The maximizing face signs were eliminated before conditional independence was used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Outer game with affine hints:", "statement": "<p>Outer game with affine hints: soundness, joint smoothness, and effectiveness.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=(E^{(1)},\\ldots,E^{(M_0)})\n\\]\nbe the nonempty uniform equation list of Proposition \\(2\\). An equation question \\(E\\) contains its ordered variable names \\(v(E,1),v(E,2),v(E,3)\\) and right-hand side \\(\\tau_E\\). Equal equation questions have identical encodings; a list-entry index is not part of the question. Variable names may repeat.</p><p>Define the nonempty affine space\n\\[\nA(E)=\n\\left\\{\na\\in\\mathbb F_2^3:\na_1+a_2+a_3=\\tau_E,\\\na_p=a_{p'}\\text{ whenever }v(E,p)=v(E,p')\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIt has dimension at most two: after identifying equal names, the sum of the multiplicities is three, so at least one multiplicity is odd and the remaining equation has rank one.</p><p>Fix integers \\(t\\ge t_0\\ge1\\). Sample \\(t\\) independent uniform list entries, giving\n\\[\nU=(E_1,\\ldots,E_t),\n\\qquad\nA_U=\\prod_{j=1}^tA(E_j).\n\\]\nChoose a uniform \\(t_0\\)-element subset \\(H\\subseteq[t]\\), and, independently for \\(j\\in H\\), uniform \\(p_j\\in[3]\\). Set\n\\[\nV_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n(\\mathrm{variable},v(E_j,p_j)),&amp;j\\in H,\\\\\n(\\mathrm{equation},E_j),&amp;j\\notin H.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe tuple order supplies coordinate \\(j\\). A variable question reveals neither its position nor its originating equation. Define\n\\[\nA_V=\\prod_{j=1}^tA_{V,j},\n\\qquad\nA_{V,j}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb F_2,&amp;j\\in H,\\\\\n\\mathbb F_2^3,&amp;j\\notin H.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nAll displayed slots are unrestricted and fresh across coordinates, even if names repeat. The only equalities in \\(A_U\\) are those within an individual factor \\(A(E_j)\\).</p><p>For the full sample \\(e\\), define\n\\[\n(\\pi_e(a))_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n(a_j)_{p_j},&amp;j\\in H,\\\\\na_j,&amp;j\\notin H.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThe verifier accepts \\(a\\in A_U,b\\in A_V\\) exactly when \\(\\pi_e(a)=b\\). The affine map need not be onto.</p><p>Every alphabet is listed lexicographically in its displayed slots. Functions on it are represented by their complete truth tables. In particular, an affine hint is the function itself, with no formula, original coefficient vector, map description, or occurrence metadata.</p><p>For \\(q\\ge0\\), the game with \\(q\\) affine hints draws independent uniform\n\\[\nL_1,\\ldots,L_q\\in D(A_V).\n\\]\nThe players receive\n\\[\n(U,\\pi_e^*L_1,\\ldots,\\pi_e^*L_q),\n\\qquad\n(V,L_1,\\ldots,L_q),\n\\]\nand the acceptance predicate is unchanged.</p><p>If the equation list has value at most \\(3/4\\), the hinted-game value is at most\n\\[\n\\bigl(1-2^{-q}(1-c)\\bigr)^{t_0},\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&lt;1\\) is from Proposition \\(2\\). For fixed \\(q\\), this can be made smaller than any prescribed positive rational by an effective \\(t_0\\), uniformly for all \\(t\\ge t_0\\).</p><p>For every actual \\(U\\), every \\(d\\ge1\\), and independent uniform \\(L_1,\\ldots,L_d\\in D(A_V)\\), conditional on \\(U\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}\\!\\left(\n\\mathcal L((\\pi_e^*L_1,\\ldots,\\pi_e^*L_d)\\mid U),\n\\operatorname{Unif}(D(A_U)^d)\n\\right)\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sqrt{(2^{4dt_0}-1)t_0^2/t}.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(d,t_0\\), this is effectively made smaller than any prescribed positive rational by choosing \\(t\\).</p><p>All games and maps are explicit finite objects. For fixed parameters, complete local enumeration and identification of canonical questions and functions require polynomial bit complexity in the equation-list input.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(5.2\\) proves hinted soundness and its uniform effective choice of \\(t_0\\); Lemma \\(5.3\\) proves joint smoothness and the subsequent effective choice of \\(t\\).</p><p>The complete outer sampling space contains\n\\[\nM_0^t\\binom t{t_0}3^{t_0}\n\\]\nequally likely draws, counting repeated list entries with multiplicity. A first alphabet has at most \\(2^{2t}\\) labels, and a second alphabet at most \\(2^{3t-2t_0}\\).</p><p>All affine spaces, functions, canonical truth tables, and maps can be enumerated by binary linear algebra and finite evaluation. For fixed parameters, local enumeration has constant size independent of the input. Comparing actual question encodings and identifying equal questions requires polynomial bit complexity. The fresh-slot and canonical-function conventions introduce no oracle or uncharged lookup table.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Consider the ordinary equation-versus-variable game:", "statement": "<p>Consider the ordinary equation-versus-variable game: sample a uniform list entry \\(E\\), choose uniform \\(p\\in[3]\\), give \\(E\\) to the first player and only \\(v(E,p)\\) to the second, and ask for \\(a\\in A(E)\\) and a bit \\(b\\). Accept when \\(a_p=b\\).</p><p>If no assignment satisfies more than \\(3/4\\) of the equation list, this game has value at most \\(11/12\\), and its ordinary \\(r\\)-fold repetition has value at most \\(c^r\\) for every \\(r\\ge0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a deterministic second strategy. Its bit for each variable name defines a global assignment. At least \\(1/4\\) of the equations are violated by this assignment. Every valid first answer on such an equation disagrees in at least one of the three positions, so a uniform position detects disagreement with probability at least \\(1/3\\). An invalid first answer is always rejected. Overall failure probability is at least \\(1/12\\).</p><p>Repeated variable names do not affect the argument: agreement at all three positions would give precisely the violated assignment. Pad the first answer alphabet to \\(\\mathbb F_2^3\\), rejecting invalid answers. The answer sizes are at most eight and two, so Proposition \\(2\\) gives the repeated bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Hint soundness.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the NO assumption of Proposition \\(5\\), for every \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(t\\ge t_0\\ge1\\), the \\(q\\)-hint game has value at most \\( \\bigl(1-2^{-q}(1-c)\\bigr)^{t_0}. \\) The bound also applies to independent shared or …", "statement": "<p>Under the NO assumption of Proposition \\(5\\), for every \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(t\\ge t_0\\ge1\\), the \\(q\\)-hint game has value at most\n\\[\n\\bigl(1-2^{-q}(1-c)\\bigr)^{t_0}.\n\\]\nThe bound also applies to independent shared or private randomness.</p><p>For fixed \\(q\\) and prescribed rational \\(s&gt;0\\), an effective \\(t_0\\) makes the bound strictly less than \\(s\\), simultaneously for every \\(t\\ge t_0\\), every equation-list size, and every completeness parameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the unrestricted slots of \\(A_V\\) to write each uniform affine hint uniquely as\n\\[\nL_\\ell(z)\n=\n\\kappa_\\ell\n+\n\\sum_{j\\in H}\\beta_{\\ell j}z_j\n+\n\\sum_{j\\notin H}\\sum_{p=1}^3\\beta_{\\ell jp}z_{jp}.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nAll coefficients and constants are independent uniform bits. They can be sampled after \\(H\\) and before any equation or position, since the abstract slots depend only on \\(H\\).</p><p>Define the active set\n\\[\nA=\\{j\\in H:\\beta_{\\ell j}=0\\text{ for every }\\ell\\in[q]\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|A|\\sim\\operatorname{Bin}(t_0,2^{-q}).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nConditional on \\(H\\), the active set and coefficient bits are independent of the equation and position samples. When \\(q=0\\), all hidden coordinates are active.</p><p>Give both players the additional background \\(W\\) consisting of \\(H\\), all coefficients and constants, and all equation samples and chosen positions outside \\(A\\). It determines \\(A\\) without inspecting active samples. Conditional on \\(W\\), the pairs \\((E_j,p_j)\\), \\(j\\in A\\), remain independent ordinary game samples.</p><p>For fixed \\(W\\), active ordinary repeated-game questions reconstruct the original information separately:</p><p class=\"li\">The first player receives the active equations. Together with \\(W\\), these reconstruct \\(U,A_U\\). Since active hint coefficients vanish,\n  \\[\n  (\\pi_e^*L_\\ell)(a)\n  =\n  \\kappa_\\ell\n  +\n  \\sum_{j\\in H\\setminus A}\\beta_{\\ell j}(a_j)_{p_j}\n  +\n  \\sum_{j\\notin H}\\sum_{p=1}^3\\beta_{\\ell jp}(a_j)_p.\n  \\]\n  Every position on the right is in the background. The player can compute the canonical truth tables without knowing an active position or variable question.</p><p class=\"li\">The second player receives active variable names. Together with \\(W,H\\), these reconstruct \\(V,A_V\\). Formula (5.3) computes the direct hints without revealing an active equation or its selected position.</p><p>No metadata is attached to a zero summand. Restricting a zero function cannot convey a hidden position. Nonzero contributions and affine offsets are determined by the background and the receiving player’s own question.</p><p>Apply the original strategies to the reconstructed information and restrict their answers to active coordinates. Full acceptance implies acceptance of the ordinary \\(|A|\\)-fold repeated game. Lemma \\(5.1\\) bounds the conditional probability by \\(c^{|A|}\\), including the trivial bound one when \\(A\\) is empty.</p><p>Coincidences of names or questions across coordinates do not alter product sampling. With \\(W\\) fixed, the simulated strategies may use all coincidences available from their own questions, as arbitrary repeated-game strategies may.</p><p>Average:\n\\[\n\\mathbb P[\\mathrm{accept}]\n\\le\n\\mathbb Ec^{|A|}\n=\n\\sum_{r=0}^{t_0}\n\\binom{t_0}{r}(2^{-q}c)^r(1-2^{-q})^{t_0-r}\n=\n\\bigl(1-2^{-q}(1-c)\\bigr)^{t_0}.\n\\]\nFixing random seeds first proves the same bound for independent randomized strategies.</p><p>Replace \\(c\\), if needed, by an effectively chosen rational \\(\\bar c\\in[c,1)\\). Repeated multiplication of\n\\[\n1-2^{-q}(1-\\bar c)&lt;1\n\\]\nfinds a \\(t_0\\) whose power is strictly less than \\(s\\), independently of \\(t\\), the instance, and its completeness error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Joint smoothness.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the game of Proposition \\(5\\), fix an actual \\(U\\), integers \\(d\\ge1,t\\ge t_0\\ge1\\), and sample the remaining outer randomness conditional on \\(U\\).", "statement": "<p>For the game of Proposition \\(5\\), fix an actual \\(U\\), integers \\(d\\ge1,t\\ge t_0\\ge1\\), and sample the remaining outer randomness conditional on \\(U\\). Draw independent uniform \\(L_1,\\ldots,L_d\\in D(A_V)\\). Then (5.5) holds uniformly in \\(U\\), including repeated names and zero-dimensional local alphabets.</p><p>For positive rational \\(\\Delta\\), it suffices to choose\n\\[\nt\\ge t_0,\n\\qquad\nt&gt;\\frac{(2^{4dt_0}-1)t_0^2}{4\\Delta^2}\n\\]\nto make the bound strictly less than \\(\\Delta\\).</p><p>The estimate may be averaged over any distribution of \\(U\\) and any extra randomness independent of the outer sample. A statistic bounded in absolute value by \\(B\\), selected as a function of \\(U\\) and some of the pulled-back forms, changes in expectation by at most \\(2B\\) times the total-variation bound when the whole joint collection is replaced by independent uniform forms. This does not assert uniformity conditional on previously revealed forms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(A_j=A(E_j)\\) and \\(r_j=\\dim A_j\\le2\\). Using \\(E_j\\) alone, choose an affine origin \\(o_j\\) and an injective linear map\n\\[\nT_j:\\mathbb F_2^{r_j}\\to\\mathbb F_2^3\n\\]\nwhose image is the translation space of \\(A_j\\). Thus \\(a_j=o_j+T_ju_j\\). Lexicographic search and binary Gaussian elimination make these choices canonical and effective.</p><p>A \\(d\\)-tuple of forms on \\(A_U\\) has \\(d\\) constant bits and linear-part groups\n\\[\n\\xi_j\\in(\\mathbb F_2^{r_j})^d.\n\\]\nGenerate the forms by the raw independent coefficients and constants in (5.3). After pullback, the constant bits are the independent original constants plus offsets determined by the remaining data. Conditional on those data, this is a translation of \\(\\mathbb F_2^d\\). Hence the constants remain jointly uniform and independent of every linear part, \\(H\\), and the positions. Their common uniform factor contributes no total-variation error.</p><p>Fix \\(H\\). If \\(j\\notin H\\), a uniform raw coefficient vector \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_2^3\\) gives linear part \\(T_j^Tw\\). This map is onto \\(\\mathbb F_2^{r_j}\\), so its output is uniform. The \\(d\\) forms give the uniform law \\(\\mu_j\\) on \\((\\mathbb F_2^{r_j})^d\\).</p><p>If \\(j\\in H\\), conditional on \\(p_j\\), the \\(\\ell\\)-th linear part is\n\\[\n\\beta_{\\ell j}v_{j,p_j},\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_{j,p}\\) is the linear part of slot \\(p\\). Average over the single shared uniform \\(p_j\\) to obtain a law \\(Q_j\\). Its density \\(\\rho_j\\) relative to \\(\\mu_j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mu_j}\\rho_j=1,\n\\qquad\n0\\le\\rho_j\\le2^{r_jd}\\le2^{2d}.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nThis permits zero or coinciding slot linear parts. If \\(r_j=0\\), \\(\\rho_j=1\\).</p><p>Fresh slots and independent coefficients and positions make the groups independent across \\(j\\). Relative to\n\\[\n\\mu=\\prod_{j=1}^t\\mu_j,\n\\]\nthe density for fixed \\(H\\) is\n\\[\ng_H(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_t)=\\prod_{j\\in H}\\rho_j(\\xi_j).\n\\]\nThe desired density is \\(g=\\mathbb E_Hg_H\\).</p><p>Let \\(H'\\) be an independent uniform \\(t_0\\)-subset. If \\(H\\cap H'=\\varnothing\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu g_Hg_{H'}=1.\n\\]\nIn general,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu g_Hg_{H'}\\le2^{4dt_0},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb P[H\\cap H'\\ne\\varnothing]\n\\le\n\\sum_{j\\in H}\\mathbb P[j\\in H']\n=\nt_0^2/t.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu(g-1)^2\n=\n\\mathbb E_{H,H'}\\mathbb E_\\mu g_Hg_{H'}-1\n\\le\n(2^{4dt_0}-1)t_0^2/t.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz yields\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(g\\mu,\\mu)\n=\n\\frac12\\mathbb E_\\mu|g-1|\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sqrt{\\mathbb E_\\mu(g-1)^2},\n\\]\nproving (5.5). The effective choice of \\(t\\) follows by squaring the desired inequality.</p><p>The final bounded-statistic assertion is the total-variation expectation bound applied to the joint collection, uniformly in \\(U\\), and then averaged. It is the content of the paper’s Remark 5.4.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, “A gap for bit comparisons.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed integer \\(J\\ge20\\), effective choices of constants give a four-test comparison system constructed from the uniform equation list of Proposition \\(2\\), with a fixed positive completeness error \\(\\zeta\\) …", "statement": "<p>For every fixed integer \\(J\\ge20\\), effective choices of constants give a four-test comparison system constructed from the uniform equation list of Proposition \\(2\\), with a fixed positive completeness error \\(\\zeta\\) chosen by the construction.</p><p>Each actual first question \\(U\\) and second question \\(V\\) of Proposition \\(5\\) receives one folded proof \\(f_U,f_V\\) on its alphabet. Equal actual questions on the same side use the same proof.</p><p>The first three tests are those of Proposition \\(3\\), applied to \\(f_V\\) under the marginal distribution of \\(V\\). The fourth samples a full outer sample \\(e\\), an independent uniform face array \\(B\\) over \\(D(A_V)\\), and the Gaussian table\n\\[\nP=P_{c+\\sigma g,\\eta\\lambda}\n\\]\non the common code \\(\\mathcal C\\), and compares\n\\[\nf_U(P(\\pi_e^*B))\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nf_V(P(B)).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nThe same Gaussian table is used on both sides, and pullback acts on every array row.</p><p>For positive rational budgets \\(b_1,b_2,b_3,b_4\\), define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cost}(f)\n=\n\\sum_{j=1}^4\\frac{\\mathbb P[\\text{test }j\\text{ fails}]}{b_j}.\n\\]\nThe effective choices satisfy:</p><p class=\"li\">In the YES case, some proof collection has cost at most \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the NO case, every proof collection has cost greater than \\(J\\).</p><p>Alphabet sizes and all local numerical parameters are bounded by effective constants depending only on \\(J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition \\(4\\) with \\(J\\), taking \\(p,\\theta\\) rational by decreasing them if necessary. Set\n\\[\nM=mn^{m-1},\n\\qquad q=M-1,\n\\qquad M_*=\\frac{\\sqrt N}{\\sigma}.\n\\]\nThe repetition counts \\(t_0,t\\) will be selected later.</p><p>Suppose in the NO case that \\(\\operatorname{Cost}(f)\\le J\\). Each first-three-test bound required by Proposition \\(4\\) holds for the weighted family indexed by \\(V\\).</p><p>Draw \\(e\\), independent uniform \\(i\\in[m],x\\in X\\), and independent standard Gaussian \\(c\\). The \\(q\\) rows other than \\(B_{x_{-i}}^i\\), ordered using only \\(i,x\\), serve as the affine hints of Proposition \\(5\\). Both players use \\(c,i,x\\) as additional independent public randomness and form\n\\[\nH_V(b)\n=\nh_{x,V}(B[B_{x_{-i}}^i\\leftarrow b],c),\n\\qquad b\\in D(A_V),\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\\[\nH_U(a')\n=\nh_{x,U}((\\pi_e^*B)[B_{x_{-i}}^i\\leftarrow a'],c),\n\\qquad a'\\in D(A_U).\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nThe first player fills the missing row of its received array; knowledge of \\(e,V\\) is unnecessary. Both functions have absolute bound \\(M_*\\).</p><p>First, these row functions are close. Apply Gaussian differentiation to the difference of the two bits in (6.1), using the same \\(g,\\lambda\\), and then Bessel’s inequality:\n\\[\n\\frac1N\\sum_x\n|h_{x,V}(B,c)-h_{x,U}(\\pi_e^*B,c)|^2\n\\le\n\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\n\\mathbb E_{g,\\lambda}\n|f_V(P(B))-f_U(P(\\pi_e^*B))|^2.\n\\]\nAveraging bounds the right side by \\(4Jb_4/\\sigma^2\\). Integrating the missing row restores an independent uniform array, so\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|H_V-H_U\\circ\\pi_e^*\\|_{L^2(D(A_V))}^2\n\\le\\frac{4Jb_4}{\\sigma^2}.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nChoose positive rational \\(b_4\\) so small that\n\\[\n\\frac{64Jb_4}{\\sigma^2\\theta^2}&lt;\\frac p4.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nBy Markov, the \\(L^2\\) norm in (6.4) is less than \\(\\theta/4\\), except with probability less than \\(p/4\\).</p><p>Next control diffuse first-side frequencies after pullback. Choose positive rational \\(u&lt;1\\). At each first-player information state, remove from \\(H_U\\) every Fourier term of magnitude at least \\(u\\), and call the remainder \\(r_U\\). Parseval gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\alpha:|\\widehat H_U(\\alpha)|\\ge u\\}\n\\le M_*^2/u^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_\\alpha|\\widehat r_U(\\alpha)|^4\\le u^2M_*^2,\n\\qquad\n\\|r_U\\|_\\infty\\le M_r:=M_*+M_*^2/u.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nFor the last inequality, each removed coefficient satisfies \\(|a|\\le|a|^2/u\\).</p><p>If Proposition \\(5\\)’s total-variation bound for \\(d=q+3\\) is at most \\(\\Delta\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{\\beta\\in D(A_V)^*}\n|\\widehat{r_U\\circ\\pi_e^*}(\\beta)|^4\n\\le\nu^2M_*^2+2\\Delta M_r^4.\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nIndeed, fix \\(U\\) and public randomness. The fourth-moment identity expresses the Fourier sum as the average of\n\\[\nr_U(\\pi_e^*a)r_U(\\pi_e^*b)r_U(\\pi_e^*c')\nr_U(\\pi_e^*(a+b+c')),\n\\]\nwhere \\(a,b,c'\\) are independent uniform forms on \\(A_V\\). The whole expression, including the choice of \\(r_U\\), depends only on the \\(q+3\\) pulled-back forms consisting of the background and these three arguments, and is bounded by \\(M_r^4\\).</p><p>Replace their joint law by independent uniform forms on \\(A_U\\). Joint smoothness changes expectation by at most \\(2\\Delta M_r^4\\). Under replacement, the background fixes \\(r_U\\), and the fourth-moment identity and (6.6) give \\(u^2M_*^2\\). This proves (6.7) without conditionally replacing a missing row against a nonuniform background.</p><p>Choose \\(u\\) sufficiently small and then positive rational \\(\\Delta\\) sufficiently small that\n\\[\n\\frac{256(u^2M_*^2+2\\Delta M_r^4)}{\\theta^4}\n&lt;\n\\frac p4.\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality makes every coefficient of \\(r_U\\circ\\pi_e^*\\) smaller than \\(\\theta/4\\), except with probability less than \\(p/4\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(4\\) supplies, with probability at least \\(p\\), an odd frequency \\(\\beta\\) of \\(H_V\\) with coefficient magnitude at least \\(\\theta\\). Combining this with the two exceptional-event bounds leaves probability at least \\(p/2\\). On that event, the coefficient of \\(H_U\\circ\\pi_e^*\\) at \\(\\beta\\) exceeds \\(3\\theta/4\\), because each coefficient difference is bounded by the \\(L^2\\) difference. The remainder contributes less than \\(\\theta/4\\). Hence the pullback of the removed part has a nonzero coefficient at \\(\\beta\\), so some removed frequency \\(\\alpha\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(\\pi_e^*)^*\\alpha=\\beta.\n\\]\nCoefficients with a common image may add, but a nonzero sum has a nonzero summand. Since \\(\\pi_e^*(1)=1\\) and \\(\\beta\\) is odd, \\(\\alpha\\) is odd.</p><p>The first player lists odd frequencies of \\(H_U\\) of magnitude at least \\(u\\); the second lists odd frequencies of \\(H_V\\) of magnitude at least \\(\\theta\\). They are local lists of labels by Proposition \\(1\\), of sizes at most\n\\[\nM_*^2/u^2,\\qquad M_*^2/\\theta^2.\n\\]\nWith probability at least \\(p/2\\), the lists contain an agreeing pair. Choose uniformly from nonempty lists, using independent private randomness, and choose a fixed label if a list is empty. Success probability is at least\n\\[\ns_*=\n\\frac{p/2}\n{(1+M_*^2/u^2)(1+M_*^2/\\theta^2)}\n&gt;0.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nThe decoder need not be efficient: it only bounds the value of a finite game. Continuous independent public randomness does not increase that value.</p><p>Choose \\(t_0\\) effectively so that the hinted soundness bound of Proposition \\(5\\), for this \\(q\\), is strictly below \\(s_*\\). Then choose \\(t\\ge t_0\\) so that joint smoothness for \\(d=q+3\\) gives the required \\(\\Delta\\). This contradicts (6.9), proving NO cost greater than \\(J\\).</p><p>Finally choose rational \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) with \\(t\\zeta\\le b_4\\), and invoke Proposition \\(2\\). Its NO bound is always \\(3/4\\), so this last choice does not affect repetition parameters.</p><p>In the YES case, take a global assignment satisfying at least \\(1-\\zeta\\) of the equations. Give each \\(V\\) its values in all requested slots. Give \\(U\\) those values when all its equations are satisfied, and any label of \\(A_U\\) otherwise. Labels agree under \\(\\pi_e\\) except with probability at most \\(t\\zeta\\). Use evaluation proofs. Lemma \\(3.1\\) bounds each of the first three normalized costs by one; agreeing labels make the fourth comparison exact, so its normalized cost is at most one as well.</p><p>The parameter order is\n\\[\nJ\\longrightarrow\n\\text{inner constants}\n\\longrightarrow\nb_4,u,\\Delta,s_*\n\\longrightarrow\nt_0\\longrightarrow t\\longrightarrow\\zeta.\n\\]\nAll strict inequalities admit rational slack and integer search. Here \\(M_*^2=N/\\sigma^2\\) is rational, and before choosing \\(\\Delta\\), the possibly irrational \\(M_r\\) may be bounded above by\n\\[\nN/\\sigma+N/(\\sigma^2u).\n\\]\nThus all constants are effective and depend only on \\(J\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Deterministic finite comparisons and numerical multiplicities.", "statement": "<p>Deterministic finite comparisons and numerical multiplicities.</p><p>Fix an integer \\(K\\ge2\\), put \\(J=10K\\), and make the choices of Proposition \\(6\\). Write \\(\\operatorname{Cost}_\\infty\\) for its Gaussian comparison cost, and set\n\\[\nW=\\sum_{j=1}^4b_j^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet \\(M_0\\ge1\\) be the uniform equation-list length.</p><p>There is a deterministic finite rational comparison system with cost \\(\\operatorname{Cost}_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\) such that, for every folded proof collection,\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Cost}_{\\mathrm{fin}}(f)\n-\\operatorname{Cost}_\\infty(f)|&lt;\\frac12.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nIts minimum cost is less than \\(9/2\\) in the YES case, and every proof has cost greater than \\(10K-1/2\\) in the NO case.</p><p>The finite system is representable by equality and inequality demands on canonical sign variables, with nonnegative integer multiplicities after multiplication by\n\\[\nD_0=C_KM_0^t,\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_K\\) is an effectively computed positive integer depending only on \\(K\\). The sum of all multiplicities is exactly \\(D_0W=O_K(M_0^t)\\).</p><p>Every assignment of the sign variables corresponds bijectively to a folded proof collection, and the weighted failure cost of the signed demands is exactly \\(\\operatorname{Cost}_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\), including demands with coincident endpoints. The full construction, including numerical preprocessing, is deterministic and polynomial-time for fixed \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(7.1\\) gives a single rational replacement of the complete joint Gaussian sign tables, uniformly valid for every proof. Applying the cost gap of Proposition \\(6\\) gives YES minimum less than \\(9/2\\) and NO minimum greater than \\(10K-1/2\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(7.2\\) supplies the explicit denominator \\(D_0\\), canonical sign variables, exact signed demands, and polynomial numerical sum of multiplicities. The local enumerations and rational-law preprocessing depend only on \\(K\\). There are polynomially many outer samples for fixed \\(t\\), and their unbounded strings are variable names from the polynomial-size equation list. Thus the complete construction is deterministic polynomial-time for fixed \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Effective finite comparisons.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use \\(X,N,\\mathcal C,\\sigma,\\eta\\) fixed by \\(K\\).", "statement": "<p>Use \\(X,N,\\mathcal C,\\sigma,\\eta\\) fixed by \\(K\\). For independent standard Gaussians \\(c,g,\\lambda\\), define\n\\[\nT_0(z)\n=\n\\operatorname{sgn}\n\\left(N^{-1/2}\\sum_xc_x(-1)^{z_x}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_1(z)\n=\n\\operatorname{sgn}\n\\left(\nN^{-1/2}\\sum_x(c_x+\\sigma g_x)(-1)^{z_x}\n+\\eta\\lambda_z\n\\right).\n\\]\nFor every prescribed rational \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/4\\), there is a deterministic rational law \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) for the pair \\((T_0,T_1)\\) whose total-variation distance from the Gaussian law is less than \\(\\varepsilon\\). Its positive integer common denominator and complete table are computable by finite work depending only on \\(K,\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Taking \\(\\varepsilon=1/(8(1+W))\\) gives (7.1), simultaneously for every proof collection.</p>", "proof": "<p>The pair takes values in a finite set of size \\(2^{2|\\mathcal C|}\\). All four tests are deterministic functions of this pair and finite random choices: the first uses both tables; the second uses one \\(T_1\\) for \\(B,B'\\); the third forces one entry of \\(T_1\\); the fourth uses one \\(T_1\\) on both alphabets.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nd=2N+|\\mathcal C|,\n\\qquad\nS=2|\\mathcal C|,\n\\qquad\nA=2N+1.\n\\]\nThe \\(S\\) threshold inputs are linear forms in a standard Gaussian vector \\(Z\\in\\mathbb R^d\\). Their variances are \\(1\\) or \\(1+\\sigma^2+\\eta^2\\), and each coefficient vector has \\(\\ell^1\\)-norm at most\n\\[\n(1+\\sigma)\\sqrt N+\\eta\\le A.\n\\]\nNo nonsingularity of their joint law is required.</p><p>Choose integer \\(T_G\\ge1\\) with\n\\[\nd/T_G^2&lt;\\varepsilon/4.\n\\]\nA second-moment bound and union bound show that the probability \\(\\alpha\\) outside \\([-T_G,T_G]^d\\) is at most \\(d/T_G^2\\).</p><p>Partition \\([-T_G,T_G]\\) into \\(L\\) equal intervals of length \\(h=2T_G/L\\), with rational midpoints, taking\n\\[\nSAh&lt;\\varepsilon/4.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nRounding an interior point to its cell midpoint changes each threshold by at most \\(Ah/2\\). A sign change requires that the original threshold have absolute value at most \\(Ah/2\\). Each threshold is Gaussian with variance at least one and density at most one, so the union probability is at most \\(SAh\\).</p><p>Couple \\(Z\\) with a vector \\(Y\\) by keeping \\(Y=Z\\) inside the cube and, outside, drawing \\(Y\\) independently from the Gaussian conditioned on the cube. Then \\(Y\\) has that conditional law. Charge probability \\(\\alpha\\) outside and the sign-change bound inside. The original table law and the law obtained by rounding the conditioned Gaussian differ in total variation by at most\n\\[\nd/T_G^2+SAh&lt;\\varepsilon/2.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]</p><p>Compute rational conditioned cell masses without a real-number oracle. Put \\(U=T_G^2/2\\). Choose integer \\(r\\ge0\\) so that\n\\[\ne_r=\\frac{U^{2r+1}}{(2r+1)!},\n\\qquad\n2dT_Ge_r&lt;\\varepsilon/4.\n\\]\nThe search terminates because\n\\[\ne_{r+1}/e_r\n=\n\\frac{U^2}{(2r+2)(2r+3)}\n\\]\nis eventually at most \\(1/2\\). Define\n\\[\nQ_r(v)=\\sum_{j=0}^{2r}\\frac{(-1)^jv^{2j}}{2^jj!}.\n\\]\nTaylor’s remainder gives\n\\[\n0\\le Q_r(v)-e^{-v^2/2}\\le e_r\n\\qquad(|v|\\le T_G).\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(Q_r&gt;0\\). For each grid interval \\(I=[a,b]\\), compute\n\\[\nw_I=\n\\frac{\\int_a^bQ_r(v)\\,dv}\n{\\int_{-T_G}^{T_G}Q_r(v)\\,dv}.\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThese are positive rationals summing exactly to one, evaluated by the rational antiderivative\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{2r}\n\\frac{(-1)^jv^{2j+1}}{2^jj!(2j+1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(g(v)=e^{-v^2/2}\\),\n\\[\nZ_g=\\int_{-T_G}^{T_G}g,\n\\qquad\nE_r=\\int_{-T_G}^{T_G}(Q_r-g).\n\\]\nSince \\(T_G\\ge1\\) and \\(e^{-u}\\ge1-u\\),\n\\[\nZ_g\\ge\\int_{-1}^1(1-v^2/2)\\,dv&gt;1,\n\\qquad\n0\\le E_r\\le2T_Ge_r.\n\\]\nThe density \\(Q_r/(Z_g+E_r)\\) is a mixture of \\(g/Z_g\\) and the normalized nonnegative residual, with residual weight \\(E_r/(Z_g+E_r)\\). Their total-variation distance is at most \\(2T_Ge_r\\).</p><p>The conditioned Gaussian on the cube is a product. Replacing coordinates one at a time bounds product-law total variation by the sum of coordinate errors. Rounding cannot increase it. Hence the rational grid law assigning mass \\(\\prod_{i=1}^dw_{I_i}\\) to a cell differs from the rounded conditional Gaussian by less than \\(\\varepsilon/4\\). Together with (7.3), the full joint sign-table error is less than \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Signs at rational midpoints are computable exactly. A \\(T_0\\) input has the sign of a rational. Multiplying a \\(T_1\\) input by \\(\\sqrt N\\) gives \\(a+b\\sqrt N\\), with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Q\\). Equal signs or zero terms are immediate; opposite signs are decided by comparing \\(a^2\\) and \\(Nb^2\\). Equality gives an exact zero, assigned sign \\(+1\\).</p><p>Choose a common denominator \\(Q_1\\) of the finitely many \\(w_I\\), for example their denominator product, and set \\(Q=Q_1^d\\). Enumerate the \\(L^d\\) cells, compute their complete sign-table pairs, and collect integer numerators for identical pairs. This gives \\(\\widehat\\mu\\) with denominator \\(Q\\), by finite arithmetic depending only on fixed parameters.</p><p>Finally take \\(\\varepsilon=1/(8(1+W))\\). For any fixed proof and fixed finite choices in a test, its failure indicator is a \\(\\{0,1\\}\\)-valued function of the joint table. Its expectation changes by less than \\(\\varepsilon\\). Averaging all other choices preserves this bound, so\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Cost}_{\\mathrm{fin}}(f)-\\operatorname{Cost}_\\infty(f)|\n&lt;\n\\varepsilon W&lt;\\frac12\n\\]\nfor every proof, with no union bound over proofs. Proposition \\(6\\) gives the asserted finite YES and NO costs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the finite system of Lemma \\(7.1\\), let \\( H_K=\\binom t{t_0}3^{t_0}, \\qquad M=mn^{m-1}. \\) Write \\(\\delta^2/m=a_0/r_0\\) in lowest terms, and let \\(Q\\) be the rational sign-table denominator.", "statement": "<p>For the finite system of Lemma \\(7.1\\), let\n\\[\nH_K=\\binom t{t_0}3^{t_0},\n\\qquad\nM=mn^{m-1}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\delta^2/m=a_0/r_0\\) in lowest terms, and let \\(Q\\) be the rational sign-table denominator. Define\n\\[\nA_K=Q\\,2^{2M(3t+1)}r_0^M|\\mathcal C|.\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nWrite \\(b_j=s_j/t_j\\) in lowest terms, with positive integers \\(s_j,t_j\\). Choose a positive integer \\(B_K\\) divisible by every \\(s_j\\), and set\n\\[\nC_K=H_KA_KB_K,\n\\qquad\nD_0=C_KM_0^t.\n\\]</p><p>Then every finite test outcome has an integer cost multiplicity after multiplication by \\(D_0\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{elementary\\ demands}}\\mathrm{multiplicity}\n=\nD_0W\n=\nO_K(M_0^t).\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]</p><p>There is an explicit canonical sign-variable representation of folded proofs for which each comparison becomes an equality or inequality demand, with exactly preserved failure cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are exactly \\(H_KM_0^t\\) elementary outer samples, each of probability \\(1/(H_KM_0^t)\\). For the first three tests, enumerate a full outer sample and ignore unused information, thereby obtaining the required second-question marginal. Repeated question encodings are identified, without appending sample indices.</p><p>Each second alphabet has dimension at most \\(3t\\), so sampling a uniform row in \\(D(A_V)\\) has denominator dividing \\(2^{3t+1}\\). Sample both the original and fresh arrays even when fresh rows are unused; likewise allow unused resampling flags and the forced codeword in every test. Together with \\(Q\\), all these denominators divide \\(A_K\\). Thus an elementary outcome has probability\n\\[\na/(H_KA_KM_0^t)\n\\]\nfor a nonnegative integer \\(a\\).</p><p>Its contribution to test-\\(j\\) cost has weight\n\\[\n\\frac{at_j}{H_KA_KM_0^ts_j}.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(D_0\\) gives the integer \\(aB_Kt_j/s_j\\). Summing before or after collecting demands gives exactly \\(D_0\\sum_jb_j^{-1}=D_0W\\), proving the numerical multiplicity bound.</p><p>For canonical variables, enumerate every actual alphabet lexicographically. Each lies in at most \\(3t\\) bit slots. Affine forms can be found by binary Gaussian elimination and enumerated by truth tables, in number at most \\(2^{3t+1}\\). All Boolean query tables can also be enumerated, in number at most \\(2^{2^{3t}}\\). For fixed \\(K\\), these are constants. Evaluate all pullbacks and \\(B(x)\\) on these tables; no smoothed derivative, Fourier decoder, or optimum proof is computed.</p><p>For each actual question on each side, introduce one sign variable for every complementary pair \\(\\{P,-P\\}\\). Nonempty alphabets ensure the two tables are distinct. If \\(a_*\\) is the first label, put\n\\[\n\\tau(P)=P(a_*),\n\\qquad\nR(P)=\\tau(P)P.\n\\]\nThe representative \\(R(P)\\) takes value \\(+1\\) at \\(a_*\\). Assign signs \\(y_{q,R}\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\), where \\(q\\) includes the side tag and actual question but no occurrence description. The bijection with folded proofs is\n\\[\nf_q(P)=\\tau(P)y_{q,R(P)}.\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nf_q(P)=f_{q'}(P')\n\\]\nbecomes\n\\[\ny_{q,R(P)}y_{q',R(P')}\n=\n\\tau(P)\\tau(P').\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nThe right side \\(+1\\) demands equality; \\(-1\\) demands inequality. This includes coincident variables: a self-equality never fails and a self-inequality always fails. Equation (7.9) preserves every demand’s failure indicator and hence the full weighted cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Path realization.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the finite comparison system of Proposition \\(7\\), there is a deterministic construction of a finite simple undirected unweighted graph \\(G\\) satisfying …", "statement": "<p>For the finite comparison system of Proposition \\(7\\), there is a deterministic construction of a finite simple undirected unweighted graph \\(G\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{OPT}_{\\mathrm{uncut}}(G)\n=\nD_0\\min_f\\operatorname{Cost}_{\\mathrm{fin}}(f).\n\\tag{7.11}\n\\]\nThe graph has \\(O_K(M_0^t)\\) vertices and edges, and its full construction, including bit complexity, is polynomial-time for fixed \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with one vertex for each canonical sign variable. For every unit of integer multiplicity of a demand (7.10), join its endpoint vertices by a path of length four for equality and length three for inequality. All internal vertices are fresh for that unit demand.</p><p>For coincident endpoints, these prescriptions are respectively a four-cycle through three fresh vertices and a triangle through two fresh vertices.</p><p>For a path of length \\(L\\), let its signs be \\(x_0,\\ldots,x_L\\), and let \\(r\\) be its number of uncut edges. Exactly \\(L-r\\) adjacent pairs have opposite signs, so\n\\[\nx_0x_L\n=\n\\prod_{i=1}^Lx_{i-1}x_i\n=\n(-1)^{L-r}.\n\\tag{7.12}\n\\]\nIf the demanded relation holds, alternating signs give \\(r=0\\) and reach the prescribed endpoint sign. If it fails, (7.12) rules out \\(r=0\\), while making exactly one adjacent pair equal and alternating elsewhere reaches the prescribed endpoint and gives \\(r=1\\).</p><p>The same calculation with \\(x_L=x_0\\) handles coincident endpoints. A four-cycle has minimum zero, as required for self-equality; a triangle has minimum one, as required for self-inequality. Thus each gadget’s conditional minimum is exactly its demand’s failure indicator.</p><p>For fixed original signs, the gadgets attain their conditional minima independently because internal vertices are disjoint. Conversely, every graph assignment pays at least the sum of the conditional minima. Minimize over original signs and use the bijection (7.9) to obtain (7.11), with no additive term.</p><p>No gadget has a loop or repeated edge. The internal vertices within a gadget are distinct; coincident-endpoint gadgets have length at least three. Every edge contains a vertex internal to its gadget, so distinct gadgets cannot repeat an edge. The graph is therefore simple, undirected, and unweighted.</p><p>There are at most \\(2H_KM_0^t\\) actual questions and a \\(K\\)-dependent constant number of sign variables per question. Each gadget introduces at most three vertices and four edges. The numerical bound (7.8) gives \\(O_K(M_0^t)\\) vertices and edges.</p><p>Canonical question and query encodings are identified by deterministic comparisons or sorting. Equality of variable names, construction of affine alphabets, and table pullback require polynomial work per outer sample. The integers in (7.7)–(7.8) have \\(O_K(1+\\log M_0)\\) bits. Explicit multiplicity expansion takes \\(O_K(M_0^t)\\) iterations, and vertex identifiers have \\(O_K(1+\\log M_0)\\) bits. This accounts for the full vertex and edge lists and all integer arithmetic.</p>"}], "edges": [[3, 2, "part"], [4, 2, "part"], [5, 2, "part"], [7, 6, "part"], [8, 6, "part"], [10, 9, "part"], [11, 9, "part"], [13, 12, "part"], [14, 12, "part"], [15, 12, "part"], [16, 12, "part"], [17, 12, "part"], [18, 12, "part"], [20, 19, "part"], [21, 19, "part"], [22, 19, "part"], [25, 24, "part"], [26, 24, "part"]]});
